1
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,043
ANNOUNCER: October is inventory time.

2
00:01:44,128 --> 00:01:47,721
So right now, Statler Toyota is making
the best deals of the year...

3
00:01:47,810 --> 00:01:49,941
...on all 1985-model Toyotas.

4
00:01:50,014 --> 00:01:51,728
You won't find a better car...

5
00:01:51,854 --> 00:01:55,610
...at a better price with better service
anywhere in Hill Valley.

6
00:02:01,346 --> 00:02:04,174
NEWSCASTER: The Senate is expected
to vote on this today.

7
00:02:04,261 --> 00:02:08,571
In other news, officials at
the Pacific Nuclear research facility...

8
00:02:08,693 --> 00:02:11,699
...have denied the rumor
that a case of missing plutonium...

9
00:02:11,790 --> 00:02:14,667
...was in fact stolen
from their vault two weeks ago.

10
00:02:14,755 --> 00:02:18,513
A Libyan terrorist group had claimed
responsibility for the alleged theft.

11
00:02:18,639 --> 00:02:23,608
However, officials now attribute
the discrepancy to a simple clerical error.

12
00:02:23,732 --> 00:02:27,415
The FBI, which is investigating the matter,
had no comment.

13
00:02:34,425 --> 00:02:35,426
[Buzzer blares]

14
00:03:09,309 --> 00:03:10,486
MARTY: Doc?

15
00:03:13,239 --> 00:03:14,330
Doc?

16
00:03:15,543 --> 00:03:17,584
Hello! Anybody home?

17
00:03:17,711 --> 00:03:19,048
Einstein, come here, boy.

18
00:03:19,126 --> 00:03:20,302
[Marty whistles]

19
00:03:20,390 --> 00:03:22,272
MARTY: What's going on? God.

20
00:03:23,933 --> 00:03:25,112
Jesus!

21
00:03:25,686 --> 00:03:27,566
That is disgusting.

22
00:03:27,830 --> 00:03:29,406
[Marty muttering]

23
00:03:32,588 --> 00:03:34,215
[Clocks ticking]

24
00:03:35,429 --> 00:03:37,020
[Machine humming]

25
00:03:47,749 --> 00:03:49,468
[Humming grows louder]

26
00:03:52,432 --> 00:03:54,023
[Humming grows very loud]

27
00:03:54,186 --> 00:03:55,900
[Dial clicking]

28
00:04:26,437 --> 00:04:28,155
[Electric guitar note plays]

29
00:04:29,071 --> 00:04:30,410
[Marty grunts]

30
00:04:37,923 --> 00:04:39,887
[Marty groaning]

31
00:04:53,424 --> 00:04:54,926
Rock 'n' roll.

32
00:04:55,050 --> 00:04:56,855
[Alarm bell ringing]

33
00:05:03,077 --> 00:05:04,953
MARTY: Yo.
DOC: Marty, is that you?

34
00:05:05,206 --> 00:05:07,084
Doc. Where are you?

35
00:05:07,172 --> 00:05:10,601
DOC: Thank God I've found you. Meet me
at Twin Pines Mall tonight at 1:15.

36
00:05:10,728 --> 00:05:13,193
I made a breakthrough.
I'll need your assistance.

37
00:05:13,318 --> 00:05:16,572
1:15 in the morning?
Doc, what's going on?

38
00:05:16,662 --> 00:05:18,615
MARTY: Where you been all week?
DOC: Working.

39
00:05:18,742 --> 00:05:21,834
MARTY: Is Einstein with you?
DOC: Yeah, he's right here.

40
00:05:21,920 --> 00:05:24,387
You left your equipment on all week.

41
00:05:24,512 --> 00:05:27,054
DOC: My equipment. That reminds me, Marty.

42
00:05:27,179 --> 00:05:31,112
Do not hook up to the amplifier.
There's a slight possibility of overload.

43
00:05:31,198 --> 00:05:33,491
Yeah. I'll keep that in mind.

44
00:05:33,578 --> 00:05:35,955
DOC: Good. I'll see you tonight. Don't forget.

45
00:05:36,044 --> 00:05:38,962
DOC: 1:15 a.m., Twin Pines Mall.
MARTY: Right.

46
00:05:39,087 --> 00:05:41,803
[Clocks all ringing, chiming, cuckooing]

47
00:05:45,648 --> 00:05:50,293
DOC: Are those my clocks I hear?
MARTY: Yeah, it's 8:00.

48
00:05:50,795 --> 00:05:52,835
DOC: Perfect! My experiment worked!

49
00:05:52,960 --> 00:05:56,141
They're all exactly 25 minutes slow!

50
00:05:56,516 --> 00:05:58,982
Wait a minute, Doc.

51
00:05:59,058 --> 00:06:01,362
Are you telling me that it's 8:25?

52
00:06:01,437 --> 00:06:03,654
DOC: Precisely.
MARTY: Damn!

53
00:06:04,618 --> 00:06:06,496
I'm late for school.

54
00:06:06,620 --> 00:06:09,176
[The Power of Love playing]

55
00:07:06,074 --> 00:07:08,913
MARTY: Jennifer.
JENNIFER: Strickland's looking for you.

56
00:07:09,003 --> 00:07:11,668
If you get caught,
it'll be four tardies in a row.

57
00:07:13,348 --> 00:07:15,638
All right, come on. I think we're safe.

58
00:07:15,725 --> 00:07:20,283
This time it wasn't my fault.
The doc set all his clocks 25 minutes slow.

59
00:07:20,358 --> 00:07:21,623
The doc?

60
00:07:21,698 --> 00:07:25,918
Am I to understand you're still hanging
around with Dr. Emmett Brown, McFly?

61
00:07:26,717 --> 00:07:28,759
Tardy slip for you, Miss Parker.

62
00:07:29,425 --> 00:07:32,604
And one for you, McFly.
I believe that's four in a row.

63
00:07:33,142 --> 00:07:36,111
Let me give you a nickel's worth
of free advice, young man.

64
00:07:36,197 --> 00:07:39,379
This so-called Dr. Brown is dangerous.
He's a real nutcase.

65
00:07:39,453 --> 00:07:42,085
Hang around with him,
you'll end up in big trouble.

66
00:07:42,169 --> 00:07:43,759
Yes, sir.

67
00:07:43,970 --> 00:07:47,140
You got a real attitude problem.
You're a slacker.

68
00:07:47,817 --> 00:07:51,738
You remind me of your father
when he went here. He was a slacker, too.

69
00:07:52,038 --> 00:07:54,076
Can I go now, Mr. Strickland?

70
00:07:54,202 --> 00:07:57,797
Your band is on the roster for
the dance auditions after school today.

71
00:07:57,922 --> 00:08:00,300
Why even bother?
You don't have a chance.

72
00:08:00,424 --> 00:08:02,304
You're too much like your old man.

73
00:08:02,430 --> 00:08:06,361
No McFly ever amounted to anything
in the history of Hill Valley.

74
00:08:08,291 --> 00:08:10,581
Yeah, well, history is going to change.

75
00:08:12,758 --> 00:08:14,172
Next, please.

76
00:08:15,299 --> 00:08:17,015
[Synthesizer playing]

77
00:08:23,490 --> 00:08:25,043
All right.

78
00:08:25,668 --> 00:08:28,672
We're the Pinheads.

79
00:08:30,014 --> 00:08:31,517
MARTY: One, two, three.

80
00:08:31,642 --> 00:08:33,682
[Playing Power of Love]

81
00:08:51,149 --> 00:08:54,154
Okay, that's enough. Thank you, fellas.

82
00:08:54,278 --> 00:08:56,409
Hold it, fellas.

83
00:08:56,872 --> 00:08:59,176
I'm afraid you're just too darn loud.

84
00:08:59,964 --> 00:09:01,506
Next, please.

85
00:09:03,681 --> 00:09:05,560
Next group, please.

86
00:09:08,153 --> 00:09:10,906
ANNOUNCER: Re-elect Mayor Goldie Wilson.

87
00:09:10,993 --> 00:09:13,037
Progress is his middle name.

88
00:09:13,161 --> 00:09:15,052
MARTY: "Too loud." I can't believe it.

89
00:09:15,125 --> 00:09:17,881
We'll never get a chance
to play in front of anybody.

90
00:09:17,968 --> 00:09:20,435
Marty, one rejection
isn't the end of the world.

91
00:09:20,521 --> 00:09:22,815
I just don't think I'm cut out for music.

92
00:09:22,940 --> 00:09:26,532
But you're really good,
and your audition tape is great.

93
00:09:26,659 --> 00:09:28,963
You've got to send it in
to the record company.

94
00:09:29,039 --> 00:09:30,840
-It's like Doc's always saying.
-I know.

95
00:09:30,928 --> 00:09:34,095
If you put your mind to it,
you can accomplish anything.

96
00:09:34,598 --> 00:09:36,401
That's good advice.

97
00:09:36,525 --> 00:09:40,194
All right. What if I send in the tape
and they don't like it?

98
00:09:40,279 --> 00:09:42,159
What if they say I'm no good?

99
00:09:42,286 --> 00:09:44,338
What if they say, "You got no future"?

100
00:09:44,464 --> 00:09:46,845
I can't take that kind of rejection.

101
00:09:48,056 --> 00:09:49,936
I'm starting to sound like my old man.

102
00:09:50,061 --> 00:09:52,027
-He's not that bad.
-Save the clock tower.

103
00:09:52,152 --> 00:09:54,905
He's letting you borrow the car
tomorrow night.

104
00:09:56,121 --> 00:09:57,999
Check out that 4x4.

105
00:09:58,534 --> 00:10:01,217
MARTY: That is hot.
ATTENDANT: Back her up.

106
00:10:01,292 --> 00:10:04,635
Someday, Jennifer. Someday.

107
00:10:05,185 --> 00:10:08,355
Wouldn't it be great
to take that truck up to the lake?

108
00:10:09,692 --> 00:10:12,483
Throw a couple of sleeping bags
in the back.

109
00:10:14,040 --> 00:10:16,004
Lie out underneath the stars.

110
00:10:16,079 --> 00:10:17,882
-Stop it.
-What?

111
00:10:18,759 --> 00:10:21,313
Does your mom know?
About tomorrow night?

112
00:10:21,388 --> 00:10:25,447
No, get out of town. My mom thinks
I'm going camping with the guys.

113
00:10:25,532 --> 00:10:28,537
My mother would freak out
if she knew I was going with you.

114
00:10:28,613 --> 00:10:30,578
I'd get the standard lecture about...

115
00:10:30,667 --> 00:10:33,207
...how she never did that stuff
when she was a kid.

116
00:10:33,333 --> 00:10:36,100
Look, I think the woman was born a nun.

117
00:10:36,176 --> 00:10:38,894
She's just trying to keep you respectable.

118
00:10:38,980 --> 00:10:41,108
She's not doing a very good job.

119
00:10:41,234 --> 00:10:42,484
Terrible.

120
00:10:42,572 --> 00:10:46,167
WOMAN: Save the clock tower.
Save the clock tower.

121
00:10:46,292 --> 00:10:50,349
Mayor Wilson is sponsoring
an initiative to replace that clock.

122
00:10:50,425 --> 00:10:54,819
Thirty years ago, lightning struck that
clock tower and the clock hasn't run since.

123
00:10:54,946 --> 00:10:59,741
We at the Hill Valley Preservation Society
think it should be preserved the way it is...

124
00:10:59,829 --> 00:11:01,957
...as part of our history and heritage.

125
00:11:02,045 --> 00:11:03,836
MARTY: There's a quarter.
WOMAN: Thanks.

126
00:11:03,961 --> 00:11:05,839
WOMAN: Don't forget to take a flyer.

127
00:11:05,964 --> 00:11:07,855
Save the clock tower!

128
00:11:09,732 --> 00:11:10,735
Where were we?

129
00:11:10,896 --> 00:11:11,897
[Car horn honks]

130
00:11:12,023 --> 00:11:14,240
JENNIFER: Right about here.
MAN: Jennifer!

131
00:11:17,170 --> 00:11:19,549
It's my dad. I've got to go.

132
00:11:19,636 --> 00:11:20,887
MARTY: I'll call you tonight.

133
00:11:21,014 --> 00:11:22,265
I'll be at my grandma's.

134
00:11:22,390 --> 00:11:24,769
Here, let me give you the number.

135
00:11:29,828 --> 00:11:30,830
'Bye.

136
00:11:30,905 --> 00:11:33,211
[Power of Love music playing]

137
00:12:04,213 --> 00:12:05,625
[Dog barking]

138
00:12:14,153 --> 00:12:17,158
RADIO DISPATCHER:
Bravo, Tango, Delta 6-2-9.

139
00:12:17,248 --> 00:12:20,002
Tow for impoundment.
Any unit, please respond.

140
00:12:22,756 --> 00:12:25,224
MARTY: Perfect. Just perfect.

141
00:12:29,480 --> 00:12:31,284
[Biff speaking muffled]

142
00:12:31,820 --> 00:12:33,951
BIFF: I can't believe you'd loan me your car...

143
00:12:34,036 --> 00:12:36,167
...without telling me it had a blind spot.

144
00:12:36,290 --> 00:12:37,883
I could've been killed.

145
00:12:37,969 --> 00:12:40,433
Now, Biff, I never noticed...

146
00:12:40,560 --> 00:12:44,404
...that the car had any blind spot before
when I would drive it.

147
00:12:44,481 --> 00:12:45,907
Hi, son.

148
00:12:45,981 --> 00:12:47,785
BIFF: Are you blind, McFly?

149
00:12:47,872 --> 00:12:50,163
How else do you explain
that wreck out there?

150
00:12:50,251 --> 00:12:53,507
MR. MCFLY: Biff, can I assume...

151
00:12:53,669 --> 00:12:56,687
...that your insurance
is going to pay for the damage?

152
00:12:56,763 --> 00:12:59,066
My insurance? It's your car.

153
00:12:59,192 --> 00:13:02,111
Your insurance should pay.
Who's going to pay for this?

154
00:13:02,196 --> 00:13:06,255
I spilled beer when the car smashed
into me. Who'll pay my cleaning bill?

155
00:13:06,329 --> 00:13:08,220
And where's my reports?

156
00:13:08,342 --> 00:13:12,639
I haven't finished those up yet,
but I figured since they weren't due 'til...

157
00:13:12,728 --> 00:13:15,645
Hello? Hello? Anybody home?

158
00:13:16,358 --> 00:13:18,823
Think, McFly. Think!

159
00:13:18,912 --> 00:13:20,788
I got to have time to get them retyped.

160
00:13:20,914 --> 00:13:25,136
Do you realize what would happen
if I hand in my reports in your handwriting?

161
00:13:25,222 --> 00:13:26,801
I'll get fired.

162
00:13:26,890 --> 00:13:29,568
You wouldn't want that to happen,
would you?

163
00:13:29,642 --> 00:13:31,609
-Would you?
-Of course not.

164
00:13:31,697 --> 00:13:34,162
I wouldn't want that to happen.

165
00:13:34,288 --> 00:13:36,744
Now, look.
I'll finish those reports tonight...

166
00:13:36,830 --> 00:13:40,087
...and I'll run them on over
first thing tomorrow.

167
00:13:40,175 --> 00:13:42,227
Not too early. I sleep in Saturday.

168
00:13:42,302 --> 00:13:44,182
Your shoe's untied.

169
00:13:44,519 --> 00:13:46,898
Don't be so gullible, McFly.

170
00:13:47,022 --> 00:13:48,902
Got the place fixed up nice.

171
00:13:51,041 --> 00:13:53,171
I have your car towed to your house...

172
00:13:53,245 --> 00:13:55,639
...and all you got for me is lite beer?

173
00:14:02,823 --> 00:14:04,951
What are you looking at, butthead?

174
00:14:07,295 --> 00:14:09,171
Say hi to your mom for me.

175
00:14:12,141 --> 00:14:14,182
I know what you're going to say, son...

176
00:14:14,308 --> 00:14:15,933
...and you're right.

177
00:14:16,058 --> 00:14:17,437
You're right.

178
00:14:18,024 --> 00:14:19,026
But...

179
00:14:19,825 --> 00:14:21,866
Biff just happens to be my supervisor...

180
00:14:21,956 --> 00:14:26,752
...and I'm afraid I'm just not
very good at confrontations.

181
00:14:27,766 --> 00:14:29,556
But the car, Dad.

182
00:14:29,642 --> 00:14:31,145
[Car driving away]

183
00:14:31,346 --> 00:14:33,741
MARTY: I mean, he wrecked it.
He totaled it.

184
00:14:34,942 --> 00:14:36,994
I needed that car tomorrow night, Dad.

185
00:14:37,117 --> 00:14:40,378
Do you have any idea
how important this was to me?

186
00:14:40,500 --> 00:14:43,053
MR. MCFLY: I know, and all I can say is, I'm...

187
00:14:43,718 --> 00:14:45,185
I'm sorry.

188
00:14:45,470 --> 00:14:47,599
MR. MCFLY: Believe me, Marty, you're better off...

189
00:14:47,725 --> 00:14:50,819
...without having to worry
about all the aggravation...

190
00:14:50,940 --> 00:14:54,211
...and headaches of playing at that dance.

191
00:14:54,288 --> 00:14:58,216
DAVID: He's absolutely right.
The last thing you need is headaches.

192
00:14:59,346 --> 00:15:01,146
[Mr. McFly laughing]

193
00:15:01,272 --> 00:15:02,975
[David laughing]

194
00:15:04,778 --> 00:15:06,318
[TV audience applauding]

195
00:15:06,406 --> 00:15:09,999
Kids, we're going to have to
eat this cake by ourselves.

196
00:15:10,626 --> 00:15:13,005
Your Uncle Joey didn't make parole again.

197
00:15:14,007 --> 00:15:15,260
[Mrs. McFly sighs]

198
00:15:15,385 --> 00:15:18,652
It would be nice
if you all dropped him a line.

199
00:15:18,814 --> 00:15:20,441
MARTY: Uncle "Jailbird" Joey?

200
00:15:20,568 --> 00:15:22,119
He's your brother, Mom.

201
00:15:22,195 --> 00:15:25,964
Yeah. It's a major embarrassment,
having an uncle in prison.

202
00:15:26,217 --> 00:15:28,344
We all make mistakes in life, children.

203
00:15:28,420 --> 00:15:30,134
DAVID: Goddamn it. I'm late.

204
00:15:30,223 --> 00:15:31,850
MRS. MCFLY: David, watch your mouth!

205
00:15:31,977 --> 00:15:34,527
You come here and kiss your mother
before you go.

206
00:15:34,653 --> 00:15:37,736
Come on, Mom.
Make it fast. I'll miss my bus.

207
00:15:38,196 --> 00:15:39,375
DAVID: See you later, Pop.

208
00:15:39,502 --> 00:15:40,501
[David exclaims]

209
00:15:40,627 --> 00:15:42,506
Time to change that oil.

210
00:15:42,719 --> 00:15:43,969
[Laughing]

211
00:15:45,673 --> 00:15:46,685
LINDA: Marty.

212
00:15:47,438 --> 00:15:49,227
I'm not your answering service.

213
00:15:49,315 --> 00:15:51,694
While you were outside
pouting over the car...

214
00:15:51,819 --> 00:15:53,787
...Jennifer Parker called you twice.

215
00:15:53,861 --> 00:15:55,414
MRS. MCFLY: I don't like her.

216
00:15:55,500 --> 00:15:58,756
Any girl who calls up a boy
is just asking for trouble.

217
00:15:58,882 --> 00:16:01,761
LINDA: Mom. There's nothing wrong
with calling a boy.

218
00:16:01,848 --> 00:16:05,694
I think it's terrible. Girls chasing boys.

219
00:16:05,768 --> 00:16:09,111
When I was your age,
I never chased a boy or called a boy or...

220
00:16:09,201 --> 00:16:11,753
...sat in a parked car with a boy.

221
00:16:12,416 --> 00:16:15,049
LINDA: Then how am I ever
supposed to meet anybody?

222
00:16:15,972 --> 00:16:18,267
Well, it'll just happen.

223
00:16:18,479 --> 00:16:20,770
Like the way I met your father.

224
00:16:20,896 --> 00:16:23,988
That was so stupid.
Grandpa hit him with the car.

225
00:16:24,323 --> 00:16:25,790
It was meant to be.

226
00:16:25,916 --> 00:16:26,917
[Linda sighs]

227
00:16:27,044 --> 00:16:28,382
Anyway...

228
00:16:28,797 --> 00:16:32,212
...if Grandpa hadn't hit him,
then none of you would have been born.

229
00:16:32,302 --> 00:16:33,892
Yeah, well.

230
00:16:33,979 --> 00:16:37,395
I don't understand what Dad was doing
in the middle of the street.

231
00:16:37,522 --> 00:16:39,988
MRS. MCFLY:
What was it, George? Bird-watching?

232
00:16:40,078 --> 00:16:42,618
What, Lorraine? What?

233
00:16:42,706 --> 00:16:45,636
Anyway, your Grandpa
hit him with the car...

234
00:16:45,712 --> 00:16:47,840
...and brought him into the house.

235
00:16:48,802 --> 00:16:50,680
He seemed so helpless.

236
00:16:53,487 --> 00:16:56,494
Like a little lost puppy,
and my heart just went out to him.

237
00:16:56,581 --> 00:16:59,749
LINDA: Yeah, we know.
You've told us this story a million times.

238
00:16:59,834 --> 00:17:01,249
You felt sorry for him.

239
00:17:01,336 --> 00:17:04,430
You decided to go with him
to the Fish Under the Sea dance.

240
00:17:04,519 --> 00:17:07,525
No. It was
the Enchantment Under the Sea dance.

241
00:17:08,438 --> 00:17:11,531
Our first date. I'll never forget it.

242
00:17:12,031 --> 00:17:15,711
It was the night of that terrible
thunderstorm, remember, George?

243
00:17:15,801 --> 00:17:17,676
[Indistinct chatter on TV]

244
00:17:18,391 --> 00:17:21,974
Your father kissed me
for the first time on that dance floor.

245
00:17:24,240 --> 00:17:26,367
It was then that I realized...

246
00:17:26,493 --> 00:17:29,657
...that I was going to spend
the rest of my life with him.

247
00:17:29,784 --> 00:17:31,678
[Mr. McFly laughing]

248
00:17:33,339 --> 00:17:34,719
Oh, no.

249
00:17:40,276 --> 00:17:41,907
[Music playing on radio]

250
00:17:51,936 --> 00:17:53,475
[Telephone ringing]

251
00:17:57,068 --> 00:17:58,246
[Sighing]

252
00:17:58,446 --> 00:17:59,535
Hello.

253
00:17:59,661 --> 00:18:02,379
DOC: Marty, you didn't fall asleep, did you?
MARTY: Doc.

254
00:18:03,630 --> 00:18:06,348
No. No, don't be silly.

255
00:18:06,511 --> 00:18:09,401
DOC: Listen, this is very important.
I forgot my video camera.

256
00:18:09,477 --> 00:18:12,319
Can you pick it up at my place
on your way to the mall?

257
00:18:12,407 --> 00:18:15,075
Yeah. I'm on my way.

258
00:18:39,388 --> 00:18:42,409
Einstein! Where's the doc, boy?

259
00:18:42,534 --> 00:18:43,910
[Einstein whining]

260
00:18:43,986 --> 00:18:45,490
[Machine humming]

261
00:18:48,505 --> 00:18:49,920
[Gate unlocking]

262
00:18:55,982 --> 00:18:57,859
[Mysterious instrumental music]

263
00:19:08,225 --> 00:19:09,392
[Engine revving]

264
00:19:09,479 --> 00:19:11,357
[Dramatic instrumental music]

265
00:19:32,456 --> 00:19:33,995
[Steam rushing]

266
00:19:36,839 --> 00:19:37,927
Doc!

267
00:19:39,265 --> 00:19:41,482
-Marty! You made it!
-Yeah.

268
00:19:41,810 --> 00:19:46,203
Welcome to my latest experiment. This is
the one I've been waiting for all my life.

269
00:19:46,278 --> 00:19:47,870
Well, it's a DeLorean...

270
00:19:47,956 --> 00:19:51,877
All your questions will be answered.
Roll tape and we'll proceed.

271
00:19:51,961 --> 00:19:54,342
-Is that a Devo suit?
-Never mind that now.

272
00:19:54,430 --> 00:19:56,647
DOC: Not now.
MARTY: I'm ready.

273
00:19:57,269 --> 00:20:01,202
Good evening. I'm Dr. Emmett Brown.
I'm in the Twin Pines Mall parking lot.

274
00:20:01,329 --> 00:20:04,908
It's Saturday morning,
October 26, 1985, 1:18 a.m.

275
00:20:04,998 --> 00:20:07,215
This is temporal experiment number one.

276
00:20:07,341 --> 00:20:10,305
DOC: Come on, Einie. Get in there.

277
00:20:10,472 --> 00:20:12,435
In you go. Sit down.

278
00:20:12,722 --> 00:20:14,777
Put your seat belt on.

279
00:20:16,316 --> 00:20:18,033
[Einstein grunting]

280
00:20:20,497 --> 00:20:21,876
MARTY: Okay.

281
00:20:23,214 --> 00:20:25,844
Please note that Einstein's clock...

282
00:20:25,932 --> 00:20:28,900
...is in precise synchronization
with my control watch.

283
00:20:28,974 --> 00:20:31,193
DOC: Got it?
MARTY: Right. Check, Doc.

284
00:20:33,784 --> 00:20:36,875
DOC: Have a good trip, Einstein.
Watch your head.

285
00:20:42,435 --> 00:20:45,104
MARTY:
You got that thing hooked up to the car?

286
00:20:45,189 --> 00:20:47,155
DOC: Watch this.
MARTY: Yeah. Okay.

287
00:20:47,282 --> 00:20:48,696
MARTY: Got it.

288
00:20:52,538 --> 00:20:53,627
Jesus!

289
00:20:58,600 --> 00:21:00,228
Not me! The car!

290
00:21:02,193 --> 00:21:03,910
[Tires squealing]

291
00:21:10,633 --> 00:21:12,186
[Engine revving]

292
00:21:17,658 --> 00:21:20,113
If my calculations are correct...

293
00:21:20,498 --> 00:21:23,128
...when this baby hits 88 miles per hour...

294
00:21:23,580 --> 00:21:25,970
...you're going to see some serious shit.

295
00:21:28,175 --> 00:21:29,190
[Remote control beeping]

296
00:21:29,516 --> 00:21:31,140
[Tires squealing]

297
00:21:31,643 --> 00:21:33,071
[Remote control beeping]

298
00:21:33,157 --> 00:21:34,412
[Tires squealing]

299
00:21:34,661 --> 00:21:35,662
[Remote control beeping]

300
00:21:35,738 --> 00:21:37,542
[Tires squealing]

301
00:22:01,682 --> 00:22:03,398
Watch this!

302
00:22:05,575 --> 00:22:06,579
[Beeping growing faster]

303
00:22:17,396 --> 00:22:19,698
[Dramatic instrumental music]

304
00:22:20,400 --> 00:22:21,402
[Doc laughs]

305
00:22:21,529 --> 00:22:23,830
What did I tell you?

306
00:22:23,956 --> 00:22:27,387
88 miles per hour!

307
00:22:30,519 --> 00:22:35,402
The temporal displacement occurred
exactly 1:20 a.m. and zero seconds!

308
00:22:37,203 --> 00:22:38,993
Jesus Christ!

309
00:22:39,119 --> 00:22:41,588
Jesus Christ, Doc!
You disintegrated Einstein!

310
00:22:41,675 --> 00:22:44,215
Calm down. I didn't disintegrate anything.

311
00:22:44,392 --> 00:22:48,611
The molecular structure of both Einstein
and the car are completely intact.

312
00:22:49,186 --> 00:22:50,564
Then where the hell are they?

313
00:22:50,650 --> 00:22:53,744
The appropriate question is,
"When the hell are they?"

314
00:22:53,870 --> 00:22:58,338
Einstein has just become
the world's first time traveler.

315
00:22:58,843 --> 00:23:01,471
DOC: I sent him into the future.

316
00:23:02,522 --> 00:23:04,903
One minute into the future, to be exact.

317
00:23:04,989 --> 00:23:08,157
At precisely 1:21 a.m. and zero seconds...

318
00:23:08,283 --> 00:23:11,288
...we shall catch up with him
and the time machine.

319
00:23:13,037 --> 00:23:14,214
[Dramatic instrumental music ends]

320
00:23:14,717 --> 00:23:16,684
Wait a minute, Doc.

321
00:23:18,399 --> 00:23:21,400
Are you telling me that
you built a time machine...

322
00:23:22,491 --> 00:23:24,697
...out of a DeLorean?
-The way I see it...

323
00:23:24,823 --> 00:23:28,877
...if you're going to build a time machine
into a car, why not do it with style?

324
00:23:28,968 --> 00:23:32,221
Besides, the stainless-steel construction
made the flux dispersal...

325
00:23:32,384 --> 00:23:33,387
[Watch beeping]

326
00:23:33,509 --> 00:23:34,939
Look out!

327
00:23:36,528 --> 00:23:38,243
[Tires squealing]

328
00:23:40,248 --> 00:23:41,875
[Remote control beeps]

329
00:24:15,672 --> 00:24:17,635
[Time machine steaming and creaking]

330
00:24:18,390 --> 00:24:19,639
[Doc gasps]

331
00:24:19,890 --> 00:24:22,821
MARTY: What? Is it hot?
DOC: It's cold. Damn cold.

332
00:24:25,200 --> 00:24:26,364
[Doc laughing]

333
00:24:26,540 --> 00:24:28,755
Einstein, you little devil!

334
00:24:30,957 --> 00:24:34,803
Einstein's clock is exactly one minute
behind mine and still ticking!

335
00:24:38,359 --> 00:24:39,949
MARTY: He's okay.
DOC: He's fine.

336
00:24:40,024 --> 00:24:42,327
He's completely unaware
that anything happened.

337
00:24:42,414 --> 00:24:45,082
As far as he's concerned,
the trip was instantaneous.

338
00:24:45,169 --> 00:24:47,799
That's why his watch is exactly
one minute behind mine.

339
00:24:47,886 --> 00:24:51,643
He skipped over that minute to
instantly arrive at this moment in time.

340
00:24:51,767 --> 00:24:54,149
I'll show you how it works.

341
00:24:55,699 --> 00:24:58,704
First, you turn the time circuits on.

342
00:24:58,831 --> 00:24:59,996
[Machine powering up]

343
00:25:00,959 --> 00:25:02,336
[Machine whirs]

344
00:25:02,513 --> 00:25:06,556
DOC: This tells you where you're going,
this where you are and this where you were.

345
00:25:06,643 --> 00:25:09,111
Input your destination time on this keypad.

346
00:25:09,235 --> 00:25:12,828
Say you want to see the signing
of the Declaration of Independence.

347
00:25:13,243 --> 00:25:14,419
[Machine bleeps]

348
00:25:14,920 --> 00:25:16,961
DOC: Or witness the birth of Christ.

349
00:25:19,966 --> 00:25:22,682
Here's a red-Ietter date
in the history of science.

350
00:25:22,770 --> 00:25:25,525
November 5, 1955.

351
00:25:29,282 --> 00:25:30,784
Yes, of course.

352
00:25:30,868 --> 00:25:33,840
November 5, 1955.

353
00:25:34,504 --> 00:25:36,306
What happened?

354
00:25:37,394 --> 00:25:39,775
That was the day I invented time travel.

355
00:25:40,188 --> 00:25:41,741
I remember it vividly.

356
00:25:41,815 --> 00:25:43,867
I was standing on my toilet
hanging a clock.

357
00:25:43,958 --> 00:25:46,785
The porcelain was wet.
I slipped, hit my head on the sink.

358
00:25:46,874 --> 00:25:48,751
When I came to, I had a revelation.

359
00:25:48,876 --> 00:25:52,559
A vision. A picture in my head.
A picture of this.

360
00:25:53,059 --> 00:25:55,727
This is what makes time travel possible.

361
00:25:56,278 --> 00:25:58,068
The flux capacitor.

362
00:25:58,370 --> 00:25:59,533
Flux capacitor?

363
00:25:59,659 --> 00:26:04,304
It's taken almost 30 years and my family
fortune to realize the vision of that day.

364
00:26:04,715 --> 00:26:06,932
My God, has it been that long?

365
00:26:07,635 --> 00:26:10,189
Things have certainly changed around here.

366
00:26:11,442 --> 00:26:15,284
I remember when this was all farmland
as far as the eye could see.

367
00:26:16,786 --> 00:26:19,252
Old man Peabody owned all of this.

368
00:26:20,505 --> 00:26:24,438
He had this crazy idea
about breeding pine trees.

369
00:26:28,232 --> 00:26:31,663
This is...
This is heavy-duty, Doc. This is great.

370
00:26:32,161 --> 00:26:34,714
Does it run on regular unleaded gasoline?

371
00:26:34,841 --> 00:26:38,688
Unfortunately, no. It requires something
with a little more kick. Plutonium.

372
00:26:38,761 --> 00:26:40,976
Plutonium. Wait a minute.

373
00:26:41,103 --> 00:26:43,855
Are you telling me
that this sucker is nuclear?

374
00:26:44,068 --> 00:26:45,486
Keep rolling there.

375
00:26:45,573 --> 00:26:47,376
No, this sucker's electrical...

376
00:26:47,500 --> 00:26:51,722
...but I need a nuclear reaction to generate
the 1.21 gigawatts of electricity I need.

377
00:26:51,796 --> 00:26:55,064
You don't just walk into a store
and buy plutonium.

378
00:26:55,513 --> 00:26:57,480
Did you rip that off?

379
00:26:59,321 --> 00:27:03,254
Of course. From a group of Libyans.
They wanted me to build them a bomb.

380
00:27:03,328 --> 00:27:07,722
I took the plutonium and gave them a
bomb casing full of pinball machine parts.

381
00:27:07,848 --> 00:27:10,765
DOC: Come on. Let's get you a radiation suit.
MARTY: Jesus!

382
00:27:17,991 --> 00:27:20,043
[Breathing distorted by suit]

383
00:27:34,116 --> 00:27:35,546
[Sucking]

384
00:27:40,477 --> 00:27:42,934
It's safe now. Everything's lead-Iined.

385
00:27:43,056 --> 00:27:44,699
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

386
00:27:44,774 --> 00:27:46,739
DOC: Don't lose those tapes now.

387
00:27:46,826 --> 00:27:48,794
I need that as a record.

388
00:27:48,868 --> 00:27:51,171
Let's put this back here. There we go.

389
00:27:53,637 --> 00:27:55,340
Almost forgot my luggage.

390
00:27:55,515 --> 00:27:58,271
Who knows if they got
cotton underwear in the future.

391
00:27:58,356 --> 00:28:01,363
-I'm allergic to all synthetics.
-The future.

392
00:28:01,615 --> 00:28:03,403
-That's where you're going.
-Right.

393
00:28:03,528 --> 00:28:07,923
25 years. I've always dreamed of seeing
the future, looking beyond my years...

394
00:28:08,001 --> 00:28:10,139
...seeing the progress of mankind.

395
00:28:10,265 --> 00:28:11,429
MARTY: Why not?

396
00:28:11,519 --> 00:28:14,935
I'll also be able to see
who wins the next 25 World Series.

397
00:28:15,685 --> 00:28:16,690
MARTY: Doc.

398
00:28:18,117 --> 00:28:20,495
Look me up when you get there.

399
00:28:21,247 --> 00:28:23,801
Indeed I will. Roll 'em.

400
00:28:26,805 --> 00:28:28,184
[Doc clearing throat]

401
00:28:30,149 --> 00:28:32,990
I, Dr. Emmett Brown...

402
00:28:34,195 --> 00:28:36,873
...am about to embark
on an historic journey.

403
00:28:39,003 --> 00:28:40,880
What am I thinking of?

404
00:28:40,967 --> 00:28:43,095
I almost forgot to bring extra plutonium.

405
00:28:43,222 --> 00:28:45,776
How do I expect to get back?
One pellet, one trip.

406
00:28:45,851 --> 00:28:47,905
I must be out of my mind.

407
00:28:48,029 --> 00:28:49,620
[Einstein barking]

408
00:28:50,447 --> 00:28:52,084
What is it?

409
00:28:56,468 --> 00:28:58,507
[Ominous instrumental music]

410
00:29:00,600 --> 00:29:01,689
My God.

411
00:29:02,229 --> 00:29:05,032
They found me.
I don't know how, but they found me.

412
00:29:05,119 --> 00:29:07,327
DOC: Run for it, Marty!
MARTY: Who?

413
00:29:07,788 --> 00:29:10,004
Who do you think? The Libyans!

414
00:29:11,845 --> 00:29:13,436
[Man shouting in Arabic]

415
00:29:13,511 --> 00:29:15,062
Holy shit!

416
00:29:15,311 --> 00:29:16,689
[Gunshots firing]

417
00:29:19,069 --> 00:29:20,699
I'll draw their fire!

418
00:29:28,885 --> 00:29:30,262
Doc, wait!

419
00:29:41,757 --> 00:29:43,473
[Doc screaming]

420
00:29:44,549 --> 00:29:45,813
No!

421
00:29:46,728 --> 00:29:48,519
Bastards!

422
00:29:53,288 --> 00:29:54,830
[Shouting in Arabic]

423
00:29:59,047 --> 00:30:00,303
[Grunting]

424
00:30:00,426 --> 00:30:01,602
[Gun clicking]

425
00:30:01,766 --> 00:30:03,394
[Swearing in Arabic]

426
00:30:05,198 --> 00:30:06,361
[Grunting]

427
00:30:07,324 --> 00:30:08,489
Go!

428
00:30:08,615 --> 00:30:10,167
[Engine stalling]

429
00:30:14,639 --> 00:30:15,977
Go!

430
00:30:16,103 --> 00:30:17,518
[Tires squealing]

431
00:30:18,559 --> 00:30:20,071
[Engine starting]

432
00:30:21,325 --> 00:30:22,827
[Tires squealing]

433
00:30:28,962 --> 00:30:30,391
[Machine beeping]

434
00:30:36,576 --> 00:30:38,153
[Gunshots firing and ricocheting]

435
00:30:50,312 --> 00:30:51,488
[Shouting]

436
00:30:56,459 --> 00:30:58,753
Come on! Move! Goddamn it!

437
00:31:00,378 --> 00:31:01,716
MARTY: Jesus!

438
00:31:10,493 --> 00:31:12,458
[Dramatic instrumental music]

439
00:31:21,899 --> 00:31:23,366
Holy shit!

440
00:31:24,117 --> 00:31:26,958
Let's see if you bastards can do 90.

441
00:31:27,123 --> 00:31:28,463
[Tires squealing]

442
00:31:44,965 --> 00:31:46,379
[Rumbling over ground]

443
00:31:47,555 --> 00:31:48,810
[Screaming]

444
00:31:53,730 --> 00:31:55,245
[Crashing]

445
00:31:57,876 --> 00:31:59,415
[Chickens clucking]

446
00:32:00,041 --> 00:32:01,469
[Crickets chirping]

447
00:32:01,794 --> 00:32:03,184
[Dog barking distantly]

448
00:32:04,009 --> 00:32:05,261
[Door creaks open]

449
00:32:12,161 --> 00:32:13,663
[Car blinker clicking]

450
00:32:18,721 --> 00:32:20,100
WOMAN: Pa, what is it?

451
00:32:20,263 --> 00:32:21,724
What is it, Pa?

452
00:32:21,817 --> 00:32:25,071
Looks like an airplane without wings.

453
00:32:25,660 --> 00:32:28,753
That ain't no airplane. Look.

454
00:32:33,758 --> 00:32:35,262
[Hydraulics hissing]

455
00:32:43,288 --> 00:32:45,079
[Muffled breathing]

456
00:32:46,171 --> 00:32:47,584
[Screaming]

457
00:32:49,600 --> 00:32:50,976
Don't look!

458
00:32:51,050 --> 00:32:52,678
MARTY: Listen...

459
00:32:55,647 --> 00:32:57,363
[Cows mooing]

460
00:33:04,963 --> 00:33:06,137
Hello.

461
00:33:09,307 --> 00:33:10,570
Excuse me.

462
00:33:14,115 --> 00:33:15,743
Sorry about your barn.

463
00:33:15,868 --> 00:33:17,123
[Gunshot fires]

464
00:33:19,136 --> 00:33:22,391
It's already mutated into human form!
Shoot it!

465
00:33:24,357 --> 00:33:26,738
Take that, you mutated son of a bitch!

466
00:33:28,277 --> 00:33:29,527
[Screaming]

467
00:33:31,119 --> 00:33:32,545
BOY: Wait!

468
00:33:34,801 --> 00:33:36,391
BOY: Shoot it, Pa!

469
00:33:38,806 --> 00:33:41,811
MAN: My pine! Why you...

470
00:33:41,937 --> 00:33:43,192
[Gunshot fires]

471
00:33:43,904 --> 00:33:47,335
MAN: You space bastard! You killed my pine!

472
00:33:47,748 --> 00:33:49,800
[Dramatic instrumental music]

473
00:33:50,589 --> 00:33:51,591
[Tires squealing]

474
00:33:51,715 --> 00:33:53,594
MARTY: Okay, McFly.

475
00:33:53,682 --> 00:33:55,899
Get a grip on yourself. It's all a dream.

476
00:33:55,986 --> 00:33:58,815
Just a very intense dream.

477
00:34:01,658 --> 00:34:03,375
[Brakes squealing]

478
00:34:12,151 --> 00:34:14,191
[Mysterious instrumental music]

479
00:34:22,806 --> 00:34:24,761
[Somber instrumental music]

480
00:34:41,901 --> 00:34:45,068
MARTY: Listen, you got to help me.
WOMAN: Don't stop, Wilbur!

481
00:34:47,784 --> 00:34:49,165
It can't be.

482
00:34:51,758 --> 00:34:53,132
[Machine beeps]

483
00:34:55,222 --> 00:34:56,852
This is nuts.

484
00:34:57,689 --> 00:34:59,068
[Engine stalling]

485
00:34:59,732 --> 00:35:00,820
[Engine stalling]

486
00:35:00,984 --> 00:35:02,574
Come on.

487
00:35:03,288 --> 00:35:04,826
Perfect.

488
00:35:04,917 --> 00:35:07,468
[Machine beeping insistently]

489
00:35:35,115 --> 00:35:37,169
[Mr. Sandman playing]

490
00:35:56,638 --> 00:35:58,140
[Car honking]

491
00:36:08,207 --> 00:36:09,209
[Bell dings]

492
00:36:10,262 --> 00:36:11,891
[Gas attendants speaking]

493
00:36:16,935 --> 00:36:18,363
[Mr. Sandman continuing]

494
00:36:27,303 --> 00:36:29,095
[Boy counting]

495
00:36:42,178 --> 00:36:43,893
[Bell chiming]

496
00:36:59,809 --> 00:37:03,739
ANNOUNCER: Remember,
fellow citizens, the future is in your hands.

497
00:37:03,816 --> 00:37:07,497
If you believe in progress,
re-elect Mayor Red Thomas.

498
00:37:07,698 --> 00:37:09,750
Progress is his middle name.

499
00:37:09,877 --> 00:37:12,427
Mayor Red Thomas' progress platform...

500
00:37:12,554 --> 00:37:15,008
...means more jobs, better education...

501
00:37:15,134 --> 00:37:18,229
...bigger civic improvements
and lower taxes.

502
00:37:18,314 --> 00:37:21,995
On election day,
cast your vote for a proven leader.

503
00:37:22,121 --> 00:37:24,413
Re-elect Mayor Red Thomas.

504
00:37:27,757 --> 00:37:29,721
MARTY: This has got to be a dream.

505
00:37:39,074 --> 00:37:41,129
[Davy Crockett playing on jukebox]

506
00:37:46,763 --> 00:37:48,891
MAN: Hey, kid. You jump ship?

507
00:37:49,817 --> 00:37:52,284
-What?
-What's with the life preserver?

508
00:37:57,545 --> 00:37:59,171
I want to use the phone.

509
00:37:59,884 --> 00:38:01,388
It's in the back.

510
00:38:04,066 --> 00:38:05,395
MARTY: Brown.

511
00:38:07,449 --> 00:38:08,950
Great. You're alive.

512
00:38:12,671 --> 00:38:14,258
[Watch beeping]

513
00:38:14,849 --> 00:38:16,010
[Marty sighs]

514
00:38:17,014 --> 00:38:18,517
[Phone ringing]

515
00:38:21,195 --> 00:38:22,610
Come on.

516
00:38:29,962 --> 00:38:34,019
MARTY: Do you know where 1640 Riverside...
MAN: Are you going to order, kid?

517
00:38:35,983 --> 00:38:37,147
Yeah.

518
00:38:37,484 --> 00:38:39,115
Give me a Tab.

519
00:38:39,401 --> 00:38:41,867
I can't give you a tab
unless you order something.

520
00:38:41,993 --> 00:38:43,960
Right. Give me a Pepsi Free.

521
00:38:44,086 --> 00:38:46,803
MAN: You want a Pepsi, pal,
you're going to pay for it.

522
00:38:46,926 --> 00:38:50,021
Just give me something
without any sugar in it, okay?

523
00:38:50,235 --> 00:38:52,022
Something without sugar.

524
00:38:58,420 --> 00:38:59,872
[Door opens]

525
00:38:59,961 --> 00:39:01,141
BIFF: McFly.

526
00:39:03,515 --> 00:39:05,145
What are you doing?

527
00:39:06,109 --> 00:39:07,185
Biff.

528
00:39:07,438 --> 00:39:09,903
I'm talking to you, McFly, you Irish bug!

529
00:39:10,029 --> 00:39:13,958
GEORGE: Biff. Guys. How are you doing?
BIFF: You got my homework finished?

530
00:39:14,673 --> 00:39:17,391
Actually, I figured
since it wasn't due 'til Monday...

531
00:39:17,466 --> 00:39:20,308
Hello? Hello? Anybody home?

532
00:39:20,847 --> 00:39:22,738
Think, McFly. Think!

533
00:39:22,812 --> 00:39:24,689
I got to have time to recopy it.

534
00:39:24,778 --> 00:39:28,534
You realize what would happen if I hand in
my homework in your handwriting?

535
00:39:28,624 --> 00:39:30,335
I'll get kicked out of school.

536
00:39:30,461 --> 00:39:33,005
You wouldn't want that to happen,
would you?

537
00:39:35,183 --> 00:39:36,900
-Would you?
-Of course not.

538
00:39:37,023 --> 00:39:38,902
-No.
-I wouldn't want that to happen.

539
00:39:39,028 --> 00:39:42,960
-What are you looking at, butthead?
-Get a load of his life preserver.

540
00:39:43,086 --> 00:39:44,788
Dork thinks he's going to drown.

541
00:39:44,874 --> 00:39:46,752
BIFF: How about my homework, McFly?

542
00:39:47,879 --> 00:39:49,508
Okay, Biff.

543
00:39:49,682 --> 00:39:54,401
I'll finish that up tonight and then I'll
bring it over first thing tomorrow morning.

544
00:39:54,654 --> 00:39:58,158
Not too early. I sleep in Sundays.
Your shoe's untied!

545
00:39:58,784 --> 00:40:00,839
Don't be so gullible, McFly.

546
00:40:00,914 --> 00:40:04,683
-I don't want to see you in here again.
-Okay. All right. Bye-bye.

547
00:40:26,656 --> 00:40:29,200
-What?
-You're George McFly!

548
00:40:29,573 --> 00:40:31,452
Yeah. Who are you?

549
00:40:31,578 --> 00:40:34,258
Why do you let those boys
push you around like that for?

550
00:40:34,347 --> 00:40:35,723
They're bigger than me.

551
00:40:35,798 --> 00:40:38,104
Stand tall. Have some respect for yourself.

552
00:40:38,265 --> 00:40:42,821
If you let people walk over you now,
they'll walk over you the rest of your life.

553
00:40:42,910 --> 00:40:46,078
Look at me. You think
I'll spend my life in this slop house?

554
00:40:46,166 --> 00:40:47,953
MAN: Watch it, Goldie.
GOLDIE: No, sir!

555
00:40:48,043 --> 00:40:50,510
GOLDIE: I'll do something.
I'll go to night school.

556
00:40:50,635 --> 00:40:52,852
One day, I'm going to be somebody.

557
00:40:52,927 --> 00:40:54,818
That's right. He's going to be mayor.

558
00:40:54,893 --> 00:40:56,270
Yeah, I'm...

559
00:40:56,945 --> 00:40:58,324
Mayor!

560
00:40:59,075 --> 00:41:03,008
Now that's a good idea!
I could run for mayor.

561
00:41:03,082 --> 00:41:06,848
-A colored mayor. That'll be the day.
-Wait and see. I will be mayor.

562
00:41:06,975 --> 00:41:09,353
I'll be the most powerful man
in Hill Valley...

563
00:41:09,431 --> 00:41:11,232
...and I'm going to clean up this town.

564
00:41:11,357 --> 00:41:13,985
Good. You can start by sweeping the floor.

565
00:41:16,867 --> 00:41:19,008
Mayor Goldie Wilson.

566
00:41:19,082 --> 00:41:20,960
I like the sound of that.

567
00:41:24,643 --> 00:41:26,232
[Bicycle bell rings]

568
00:41:33,543 --> 00:41:36,964
Hey, Dad! George! Hey, you on the bike!

569
00:42:08,304 --> 00:42:10,345
He's a peeping Tom!

570
00:42:16,028 --> 00:42:17,281
Dad!

571
00:42:17,457 --> 00:42:18,999
[Horn honking]

572
00:42:23,842 --> 00:42:25,644
[Dogs barking]

573
00:42:29,940 --> 00:42:32,158
Wait a minute. Who are you?

574
00:42:33,963 --> 00:42:38,255
Stella! Another one of these damn kids
jumped in front of my car!

575
00:42:39,254 --> 00:42:41,936
Come out here!
Help me take him in the house!

576
00:42:44,478 --> 00:42:46,530
[Thunder rumbling]

577
00:42:47,494 --> 00:42:48,947
Mom?

578
00:42:49,122 --> 00:42:50,663
That you?

579
00:42:51,002 --> 00:42:52,503
LORRAINE: There, there, now.

580
00:42:52,754 --> 00:42:54,170
Just relax.

581
00:42:56,686 --> 00:42:59,354
You've been asleep
for almost nine hours now.

582
00:43:01,567 --> 00:43:04,036
I had a horrible nightmare.

583
00:43:05,076 --> 00:43:08,167
I dreamed that I went back in time.

584
00:43:08,631 --> 00:43:09,971
It was terrible.

585
00:43:10,720 --> 00:43:12,063
LORRAINE: Well...

586
00:43:12,476 --> 00:43:15,981
...you're safe and sound now
back in good old 1955.

587
00:43:17,196 --> 00:43:18,608
1955?

588
00:43:22,291 --> 00:43:23,292
[Marty gasping]

589
00:43:23,419 --> 00:43:26,048
You're my...

590
00:43:26,260 --> 00:43:28,140
My name is Lorraine.

591
00:43:28,437 --> 00:43:31,356
LORRAINE: Lorraine Baines?
MARTY: Yeah.

592
00:43:32,412 --> 00:43:33,948
But you're...

593
00:43:34,037 --> 00:43:35,539
You're so...

594
00:43:35,613 --> 00:43:38,956
You're so thin!

595
00:43:39,044 --> 00:43:42,139
Just relax, Calvin.
You got a big bruise on your head.

596
00:43:42,852 --> 00:43:44,477
[Panting]

597
00:43:45,015 --> 00:43:46,268
[Marty exclaiming]

598
00:43:46,608 --> 00:43:48,323
Where are my pants?

599
00:43:48,573 --> 00:43:50,076
Over there...

600
00:43:50,614 --> 00:43:52,242
...on my hope chest.

601
00:43:53,669 --> 00:43:56,512
I've never seen
purple underwear before, Calvin.

602
00:43:56,888 --> 00:43:59,893
Calvin?
Why do you keep calling me Calvin?

603
00:43:59,981 --> 00:44:02,735
That is your name, isn't it? Calvin Klein.

604
00:44:03,236 --> 00:44:05,542
It's written all over your underwear.

605
00:44:06,491 --> 00:44:08,870
I guess they call you Cal, huh?

606
00:44:09,245 --> 00:44:11,049
No, actually, people...

607
00:44:11,762 --> 00:44:13,340
...call me Marty.

608
00:44:13,465 --> 00:44:14,641
[Sighing]

609
00:44:15,055 --> 00:44:17,020
Pleased to meet you, Calvin...

610
00:44:18,401 --> 00:44:19,774
Marty...

611
00:44:21,204 --> 00:44:22,453
...Klein.

612
00:44:29,218 --> 00:44:32,484
-Do you mind if I sit here?
-No. Fine.

613
00:44:32,649 --> 00:44:35,202
No. Good. Fine. Good.

614
00:44:37,030 --> 00:44:39,671
That's a big bruise you have there.

615
00:44:39,835 --> 00:44:40,834
[Marty shouts]

616
00:44:40,959 --> 00:44:43,264
STELLA: Lorraine, are you up there?

617
00:44:43,388 --> 00:44:45,269
Oh, my God! It's my mother!

618
00:44:45,431 --> 00:44:48,438
LORRAINE:
Quick! Put your pants back on!

619
00:44:56,715 --> 00:44:59,003
STELLA:
Marty, how long have you been in port?

620
00:44:59,127 --> 00:45:01,597
MARTY: Excuse me?
STELLA: I guessed you're a sailor.

621
00:45:01,683 --> 00:45:04,151
That's why you wear that life preserver.

622
00:45:04,274 --> 00:45:05,653
Coast guard.

623
00:45:05,741 --> 00:45:10,122
Sam, here's the young man you hit
with your car. He's all right. Thank God.

624
00:45:10,209 --> 00:45:12,504
What were you doing
in the middle of the street?

625
00:45:12,589 --> 00:45:15,306
Don't pay attention to him.
He's in one of his moods.

626
00:45:15,381 --> 00:45:18,224
Quit fiddling with that thing.
Come in here to dinner.

627
00:45:18,313 --> 00:45:20,188
STELLA: You already know Lorraine.

628
00:45:20,315 --> 00:45:22,368
This is Milton, this is Sally...

629
00:45:22,446 --> 00:45:26,664
...that's Toby, and over there
in the playpen is little baby Joey.

630
00:45:28,679 --> 00:45:30,631
MARTY: So, you're my uncle Joey.

631
00:45:31,559 --> 00:45:33,938
Better get used to these bars, kid.

632
00:45:34,065 --> 00:45:38,284
STELLA:
Yes. Joey just loves being in his playpen.

633
00:45:38,407 --> 00:45:42,002
He cries whenever we take him out,
so we leave him in there all the time.

634
00:45:42,126 --> 00:45:44,505
Well, Marty, I hope you like meat loaf.

635
00:45:44,793 --> 00:45:47,726
-Well, listen, I really ought to...
-Sit here, Marty.

636
00:45:48,511 --> 00:45:52,909
Sam, stop fiddling with that thing
and come in here and eat your dinner.

637
00:45:53,156 --> 00:45:54,623
[Sam chortling]

638
00:45:54,748 --> 00:45:56,451
Look at it roll.

639
00:45:56,577 --> 00:45:59,595
Now we can watch
Jackie Gleason while we eat.

640
00:46:05,190 --> 00:46:07,409
[Muffled voices on TV]

641
00:46:07,695 --> 00:46:11,200
Our first television set.
Dad just picked it up today.

642
00:46:12,002 --> 00:46:13,706
Do you have a television?

643
00:46:14,167 --> 00:46:17,049
Well, yeah.
You know, we have two of them.

644
00:46:17,173 --> 00:46:19,941
Wow! You must be rich.

645
00:46:20,016 --> 00:46:24,485
Honey, he's teasing you.
Nobody has two television sets.

646
00:46:29,044 --> 00:46:30,045
[Marty exclaiming]

647
00:46:30,171 --> 00:46:33,102
I've seen this one. This is a classic.

648
00:46:33,176 --> 00:46:36,193
This is where Ralph dresses up
as a man from space.

649
00:46:36,445 --> 00:46:39,610
What do you mean, you've seen this?
It's brand-new.

650
00:46:39,950 --> 00:46:42,577
Yeah, well, I saw it on a rerun.

651
00:46:43,042 --> 00:46:44,670
What's a rerun?

652
00:46:44,959 --> 00:46:46,461
You'll find out.

653
00:46:46,547 --> 00:46:48,852
You know, Marty,
you look so familiar to me.

654
00:46:48,978 --> 00:46:50,930
Do I know your mother?

655
00:46:52,144 --> 00:46:54,111
Yeah, I think maybe you do.

656
00:46:54,273 --> 00:46:57,618
STELLA: Then I'll call her.
I don't want her to worry about you.

657
00:46:57,703 --> 00:46:58,870
You can't.

658
00:46:59,044 --> 00:47:03,337
[Stuttering]
That is, nobody's home.

659
00:47:05,855 --> 00:47:07,270
Yet.

660
00:47:09,398 --> 00:47:10,576
Listen.

661
00:47:10,702 --> 00:47:13,242
Do you know where Riverside Drive is?

662
00:47:13,368 --> 00:47:17,299
It's on the other end of town.
A block past Maple. East end of town.

663
00:47:17,589 --> 00:47:20,931
A block past Maple.
That's John F. Kennedy Drive.

664
00:47:22,434 --> 00:47:24,400
Who the hell is John F. Kennedy?

665
00:47:24,525 --> 00:47:25,990
LORRAINE: Mother?

666
00:47:26,454 --> 00:47:29,080
With Marty's parents out of town...

667
00:47:29,619 --> 00:47:32,001
...don't you think
he ought to spend the night?

668
00:47:32,125 --> 00:47:34,517
After all,
Dad almost killed him with the car.

669
00:47:34,643 --> 00:47:36,594
That's true, Marty.

670
00:47:36,808 --> 00:47:40,488
I think you should spend the night.
I think you're our responsibility.

671
00:47:40,612 --> 00:47:44,034
-Well, gee, I don't know.
-He can sleep in my room.

672
00:47:44,368 --> 00:47:45,998
MARTY: I got to go!

673
00:47:46,285 --> 00:47:48,502
Thanks very much. You were all great.

674
00:47:48,590 --> 00:47:50,057
See you all later.

675
00:47:51,222 --> 00:47:52,724
Much later.

676
00:47:53,437 --> 00:47:54,863
[Door closes]

677
00:47:56,691 --> 00:47:59,332
He's a very strange young man.

678
00:47:59,496 --> 00:48:00,874
He's an idiot.

679
00:48:01,036 --> 00:48:04,380
Comes from upbringing.
His parents are probably idiots, too.

680
00:48:05,130 --> 00:48:08,812
Lorraine, you ever have a kid
who acts that way, I'll disown you.

681
00:48:31,415 --> 00:48:32,914
[Knocking on door]

682
00:48:40,389 --> 00:48:42,031
[Electronic warbling]

683
00:48:46,875 --> 00:48:48,292
MARTY: Doc?

684
00:48:48,878 --> 00:48:50,470
Don't say a word.

685
00:48:51,723 --> 00:48:54,263
DOC: No names. I want
to know nothing about you.

686
00:48:54,351 --> 00:48:55,941
MARTY: Listen, Doc...
DOC: Quiet!

687
00:48:56,015 --> 00:48:57,656
DOC: Don't tell me anything.

688
00:48:57,782 --> 00:48:59,910
MARTY: You got to help...
DOC: Quiet.

689
00:49:00,035 --> 00:49:02,164
[Electronic warbling]

690
00:49:02,539 --> 00:49:04,506
I'm going to read your thoughts.

691
00:49:04,631 --> 00:49:08,224
Let's see now.
You come here from a great distance?

692
00:49:08,300 --> 00:49:10,767
-Yeah. Exactly.
-Don't tell me!

693
00:49:11,319 --> 00:49:14,161
DOC: You want me to subscribe
to the Saturday Evening Post.

694
00:49:14,236 --> 00:49:16,791
-No.
-Not a word now!

695
00:49:16,914 --> 00:49:18,077
Quiet.

696
00:49:20,259 --> 00:49:21,585
Donations.

697
00:49:21,672 --> 00:49:23,727
You want me to make a donation...

698
00:49:23,802 --> 00:49:26,181
...to the Coast Guard Youth Auxiliary.

699
00:49:26,766 --> 00:49:28,146
Doc...

700
00:49:28,269 --> 00:49:29,611
[Electronics grow silent]

701
00:49:29,697 --> 00:49:31,828
...I'm from the future.

702
00:49:31,914 --> 00:49:34,919
I came here in a time machine
that you invented.

703
00:49:35,420 --> 00:49:40,139
Now, I need your help
to get back to the year 1985.

704
00:49:41,605 --> 00:49:42,982
My God!

705
00:49:47,616 --> 00:49:49,831
Do you know what this means?

706
00:49:51,499 --> 00:49:55,806
It means that
this damn thing doesn't work at all!

707
00:49:55,891 --> 00:50:00,274
MARTY: You got to help me. You are the only
one who knows how your time machine works.

708
00:50:00,365 --> 00:50:01,826
[Whispering] Time machine?

709
00:50:02,364 --> 00:50:04,582
I haven't invented any time machine.

710
00:50:06,085 --> 00:50:08,127
Okay. I'll prove it to you.

711
00:50:08,590 --> 00:50:11,770
Look at my driver's license. Expires 1987.

712
00:50:11,894 --> 00:50:15,486
Look at my birthday.
I haven't even been born yet.

713
00:50:15,562 --> 00:50:17,616
And look at this picture.

714
00:50:17,829 --> 00:50:19,959
It's my brother, my sister and me.

715
00:50:20,085 --> 00:50:23,338
Look at her sweatshirt, Doc.
"Class of 1984"?

716
00:50:24,305 --> 00:50:28,345
DOC: Pretty mediocre photographic fakery.
They cut off your brother's hair.

717
00:50:29,235 --> 00:50:31,615
I'm telling the truth.
You got to believe me.

718
00:50:31,691 --> 00:50:34,533
Then tell me, future boy...

719
00:50:34,959 --> 00:50:39,053
...who's president of the United States
in 1985?

720
00:50:39,128 --> 00:50:42,645
-Ronald Reagan.
-Ronald Reagan? The actor?

721
00:50:42,773 --> 00:50:44,101
[Doc exclaims]

722
00:50:44,225 --> 00:50:46,779
Then who's vice president? Jerry Lewis?

723
00:50:47,944 --> 00:50:51,460
DOC: I suppose Jane Wyman is the First Lady.
MARTY: Wait, Doc!

724
00:50:51,533 --> 00:50:53,752
DOC: And Jack Benny is treasury secretary.

725
00:50:53,840 --> 00:50:55,180
MARTY: Listen to me.

726
00:50:55,305 --> 00:50:58,721
I've had enough jokes for one evening.
Good night, future boy!

727
00:50:58,813 --> 00:51:00,150
MARTY: No, wait, Doc.

728
00:51:00,224 --> 00:51:04,533
MARTY: The bruise on your head.
I know how that happened. You told me.

729
00:51:04,658 --> 00:51:07,213
You were standing on your toilet
hanging a clock...

730
00:51:07,338 --> 00:51:09,542
...and you fell
and hit your head on the sink.

731
00:51:09,629 --> 00:51:13,385
That's when you got the idea
for the flux capacitor which...

732
00:51:13,475 --> 00:51:16,653
...is what makes time travel possible.

733
00:51:21,376 --> 00:51:23,416
[Mysterious instrumental music]

734
00:51:29,763 --> 00:51:33,945
MARTY: There's something wrong with
the starter, so I hid it here.

735
00:51:44,477 --> 00:51:46,441
DOC: After I fell off my toilet...

736
00:51:47,478 --> 00:51:48,860
...I drew this.

737
00:51:52,829 --> 00:51:54,328
MARTY: The flux capacitor.

738
00:52:03,823 --> 00:52:05,359
[Electric crackling]

739
00:52:12,510 --> 00:52:13,977
It works!

740
00:52:14,350 --> 00:52:16,142
[Doc laughing]

741
00:52:16,354 --> 00:52:17,894
It works!

742
00:52:19,196 --> 00:52:21,575
I finally invent something that works!

743
00:52:22,665 --> 00:52:24,543
You bet your ass it works.

744
00:52:25,833 --> 00:52:28,514
We've got to sneak this back
to my laboratory.

745
00:52:28,838 --> 00:52:30,727
We've got to get you home!

746
00:52:31,429 --> 00:52:32,930
This is it.

747
00:52:35,071 --> 00:52:37,114
DOC ON TV:
Never mind that now.

748
00:52:37,201 --> 00:52:39,580
DOC: Why, that's me! Look at me!

749
00:52:39,794 --> 00:52:41,921
-I'm an old man!
-I'm Dr. Emmett Brown.

750
00:52:41,996 --> 00:52:44,050
I'm in the Twin Pines Mall parking lot.

751
00:52:44,138 --> 00:52:46,180
Thank God, I've still got my hair.

752
00:52:46,305 --> 00:52:48,270
What's this thing I'm wearing?

753
00:52:48,395 --> 00:52:50,523
This is a radiation suit.

754
00:52:50,650 --> 00:52:52,365
Radiation suit?

755
00:52:52,491 --> 00:52:55,832
Of course. 'Cause of all the fallout
from the atomic wars.

756
00:52:56,835 --> 00:52:59,840
This is truly amazing.

757
00:53:00,016 --> 00:53:02,558
A portable television studio.

758
00:53:02,895 --> 00:53:06,739
No wonder your president has to be
an actor. He has to look good on TV.

759
00:53:06,815 --> 00:53:08,705
MARTY: This is the part coming up.

760
00:53:09,956 --> 00:53:14,254
This sucker's electrical but I need a nuclear
reaction to generate the 1.21 gigawatts...

761
00:53:14,380 --> 00:53:16,343
What did I just say?

762
00:53:19,725 --> 00:53:22,817
...this sucker's electrical,
but I need a nuclear reaction...

763
00:53:22,942 --> 00:53:26,872
-...to generate the 1.21 gigawatts of...
-1.21 gigawatts!

764
00:53:28,286 --> 00:53:30,429
1.21 gigawatts.

765
00:53:32,557 --> 00:53:34,148
DOC: Great Scott!

766
00:53:36,352 --> 00:53:38,905
What the hell is a gigawatt?

767
00:53:39,569 --> 00:53:41,711
How could I have been so careless?

768
00:53:41,787 --> 00:53:43,663
1.21 gigawatts!

769
00:53:43,752 --> 00:53:46,432
Tom, how am I going to generate
that kind of power?

770
00:53:46,507 --> 00:53:48,061
It can't be done, can it?

771
00:53:48,136 --> 00:53:51,563
MARTY: Doc, look.
All we need is a little plutonium.

772
00:53:52,191 --> 00:53:56,246
DOC: I'm sure that in 1985 plutonium is
available in every corner drugstore...

773
00:53:56,322 --> 00:53:59,090
...but in 1955, it's a little hard to come by.

774
00:53:59,165 --> 00:54:02,181
Marty, I'm sorry,
but I'm afraid you're stuck here.

775
00:54:03,849 --> 00:54:05,887
Doc, stuck here? I can't be stuck here.

776
00:54:06,014 --> 00:54:08,231
I got a life in 1985.

777
00:54:08,356 --> 00:54:10,408
-I got a girl.
-Is she pretty?

778
00:54:11,073 --> 00:54:12,663
Doc, she's beautiful.

779
00:54:13,538 --> 00:54:15,668
She's crazy about me. Look at this.

780
00:54:16,043 --> 00:54:17,921
Look what she wrote here.

781
00:54:18,222 --> 00:54:19,938
That says it all.

782
00:54:20,728 --> 00:54:23,405
Doc, you're my only hope.

783
00:54:23,944 --> 00:54:27,289
Marty, I'm sorry, but the only power source
capable of generating...

784
00:54:27,414 --> 00:54:30,580
...1.21 gigawatts of electricity
is a bolt of lightning.

785
00:54:32,043 --> 00:54:34,348
MARTY: What did you say?
DOC: A bolt of lightning.

786
00:54:34,475 --> 00:54:38,230
Unfortunately, you never know
when or where it's ever going to strike.

787
00:54:40,236 --> 00:54:41,410
We do now.

788
00:54:46,707 --> 00:54:47,886
This is it!

789
00:54:48,598 --> 00:54:50,802
This is the answer.

790
00:54:51,052 --> 00:54:53,445
It says here that a bolt of lightning...

791
00:54:53,522 --> 00:54:57,825
...is going to strike the clock tower
at 10:04 p.m. next Saturday night!

792
00:55:01,456 --> 00:55:04,887
If we could somehow harness
this lightning...

793
00:55:06,103 --> 00:55:09,446
...channel it into the flux capacitor...

794
00:55:10,736 --> 00:55:12,239
...it just might work.

795
00:55:12,826 --> 00:55:16,959
Next Saturday night
we're sending you back to the future!

796
00:55:17,925 --> 00:55:19,801
All right. Saturday's good.

797
00:55:19,889 --> 00:55:23,732
I can spend a week in 1955.
I can hang out. You can show me around.

798
00:55:24,059 --> 00:55:26,902
That is out of the question.
You must not leave this house.

799
00:55:26,989 --> 00:55:28,951
You must not see or talk to anybody.

800
00:55:29,078 --> 00:55:33,372
Anything you do can have repercussions
on future events. Do you understand?

801
00:55:34,389 --> 00:55:36,265
Yeah. Sure. Okay.

802
00:55:36,966 --> 00:55:38,395
Marty...

803
00:55:38,610 --> 00:55:41,776
...have you interacted with anybody else
today besides me?

804
00:55:43,828 --> 00:55:46,998
Yeah, well, I might've sort of
bumped into my parents.

805
00:55:47,085 --> 00:55:50,590
Great Scott! Let me see that photograph
again of your brother.

806
00:55:54,397 --> 00:55:57,990
Just as I thought. This proves my theory.
Look at your brother.

807
00:55:59,081 --> 00:56:02,249
MARTY:
His head's gone. It's like it's been erased.

808
00:56:03,963 --> 00:56:06,003
Erased from existence.

809
00:56:11,688 --> 00:56:14,406
They really cleaned this place up.
Looks brand-new.

810
00:56:14,495 --> 00:56:17,912
DOC: According to my theory, you interfered
with your parents' first meeting.

811
00:56:18,038 --> 00:56:21,303
If they don't meet, they won't fall in love,
get married or have kids.

812
00:56:21,431 --> 00:56:23,721
That's why your brother's disappearing.

813
00:56:23,847 --> 00:56:27,353
Your sister will follow, and unless
you repair the damage, you're next.

814
00:56:27,440 --> 00:56:30,409
MARTY: Sounds heavy.
DOC: Weight has nothing to do with it.

815
00:56:30,495 --> 00:56:32,036
Which one's your pop?

816
00:56:32,122 --> 00:56:33,462
[Bell ringing]

817
00:56:33,538 --> 00:56:34,714
That's him.

818
00:56:35,342 --> 00:56:37,558
Okay. Okay, you guys.

819
00:56:37,719 --> 00:56:39,347
[George pretending to laugh]

820
00:56:39,437 --> 00:56:42,603
Very funny.
You guys are being real mature.

821
00:56:42,778 --> 00:56:44,570
Maybe you were adopted.

822
00:56:44,694 --> 00:56:47,248
GEORGE: Okay, real mature, guys.

823
00:56:48,162 --> 00:56:50,717
GEORGE: Okay, pick up my books.
STRICKLAND: McFly.

824
00:56:50,790 --> 00:56:52,256
That's Strickland.

825
00:56:52,971 --> 00:56:55,010
Jesus, didn't that guy ever have hair?

826
00:56:55,136 --> 00:56:57,689
Shape up, man. You're a slacker.

827
00:56:57,942 --> 00:57:00,946
Do you want to be a slacker
for the rest of your life?

828
00:57:01,110 --> 00:57:02,036
No.

829
00:57:02,537 --> 00:57:05,379
-What did your mother see in him?
-I don't know.

830
00:57:05,543 --> 00:57:09,299
I guess she felt sorry for him
'cause her dad hit him with the car.

831
00:57:09,974 --> 00:57:12,192
He hit me with the car.

832
00:57:12,315 --> 00:57:14,281
That's the Florence Nightingale effect.

833
00:57:14,406 --> 00:57:17,913
It happens in hospitals when
nurses fall in love with their patients.

834
00:57:18,037 --> 00:57:19,288
Go to it, kid.

835
00:57:20,793 --> 00:57:22,920
George, buddy.

836
00:57:23,346 --> 00:57:25,727
I have been looking all over for you.

837
00:57:25,803 --> 00:57:29,143
You remember me,
the guy who saved your life the other day?

838
00:57:30,021 --> 00:57:33,113
-Yeah.
-There's somebody I'd like you to meet.

839
00:57:37,585 --> 00:57:38,759
Lorraine?

840
00:57:39,549 --> 00:57:40,799
Calvin!

841
00:57:41,802 --> 00:57:44,571
I'd like you to meet
my good friend George McFly.

842
00:57:44,647 --> 00:57:47,914
Hi. It's really a pleasure to meet you.

843
00:57:49,117 --> 00:57:51,671
-How's your head?
-Good. Fine.

844
00:57:52,046 --> 00:57:55,463
I've been worried about you
ever since you ran off the other night.

845
00:57:55,553 --> 00:57:56,890
Are you okay?

846
00:57:56,980 --> 00:57:58,229
[Bell ringing]

847
00:57:58,305 --> 00:58:00,771
-I'm sorry. I have to go.
-Come on!

848
00:58:01,700 --> 00:58:03,489
Isn't he a dreamboat?

849
00:58:07,670 --> 00:58:10,927
-She didn't even look at him.
-This is more serious than I thought.

850
00:58:11,052 --> 00:58:14,147
Your mother is infatuated with you
instead of your father.

851
00:58:15,273 --> 00:58:16,437
Wait a minute, Doc.

852
00:58:16,523 --> 00:58:20,119
Are you trying to tell me
that my mother has got the hots for me?

853
00:58:20,243 --> 00:58:22,871
-Precisely.
-This is heavy.

854
00:58:23,085 --> 00:58:25,141
There's that word again. "Heavy."

855
00:58:25,263 --> 00:58:29,482
Why are things so heavy in the future?
Is there a problem with the Earth's gravity?

856
00:58:29,556 --> 00:58:32,901
The only way we're going to
get them to mate is if they're alone.

857
00:58:32,990 --> 00:58:35,369
You've got to get them to interact...

858
00:58:35,706 --> 00:58:37,747
...in some sort of social...

859
00:58:39,126 --> 00:58:41,427
-You mean like a date?
-Right!

860
00:58:42,018 --> 00:58:44,721
What kind of date?
What do kids do in the '50s?

861
00:58:45,349 --> 00:58:47,490
They're your parents.
You must know them.

862
00:58:47,615 --> 00:58:51,371
What are their common interests?
What do they like to do together?

863
00:58:51,746 --> 00:58:52,835
Nothing.

864
00:58:52,910 --> 00:58:56,002
DOC: Look! There's a rhythmic
ceremonial ritual coming up.

865
00:58:56,216 --> 00:58:59,647
The Enchantment Under the Sea dance!
They're supposed to go to this.

866
00:58:59,722 --> 00:59:02,739
-That's where they kiss for the first time.
-All right, kid.

867
00:59:02,864 --> 00:59:06,621
You stick to your father like glue
and make sure he takes her to that dance.

868
00:59:06,998 --> 00:59:08,875
MARTY: George, buddy...

869
00:59:09,288 --> 00:59:12,555
...remember that girl
I introduced you to, Lorraine?

870
00:59:18,602 --> 00:59:20,318
What are you writing?

871
00:59:21,496 --> 00:59:22,746
Stories.

872
00:59:23,198 --> 00:59:26,881
MR. MCFLY: Science fiction stories
about visitors...

873
00:59:27,217 --> 00:59:30,386
...coming down to Earth
from other planets.

874
00:59:32,478 --> 00:59:36,157
Get out of town!
I didn't know you did anything creative.

875
00:59:36,661 --> 00:59:39,453
-Let me read some.
-No, no.

876
00:59:39,538 --> 00:59:42,470
I never let anybody read my stories.

877
00:59:43,132 --> 00:59:44,509
MARTY: Why not?

878
00:59:45,686 --> 00:59:49,444
What if they didn't like them?
What if they told me I was no good?

879
00:59:51,359 --> 00:59:54,878
I guess that would be pretty hard
for somebody to understand.

880
00:59:55,579 --> 00:59:58,132
No. No, not hard at all.

881
00:59:59,387 --> 01:00:01,940
So anyway, George. About Lorraine.

882
01:00:03,103 --> 01:00:04,695
She really likes you.

883
01:00:05,908 --> 01:00:08,664
She told me to tell you
that she wants you to ask her...

884
01:00:08,738 --> 01:00:10,705
...to the Enchantment
Under the Sea dance.

885
01:00:10,791 --> 01:00:11,792
-Really?
-Yeah.

886
01:00:12,044 --> 01:00:14,886
All you got to do is go over there
and ask her.

887
01:00:16,052 --> 01:00:18,268
Right here, right now, in the cafeteria?

888
01:00:18,354 --> 01:00:22,038
What if she said no? I don't know
if I could take that kind of rejection.

889
01:00:23,739 --> 01:00:27,670
Besides, I think she'd rather go
with somebody else.

890
01:00:29,008 --> 01:00:30,172
MARTY: Who?

891
01:00:32,104 --> 01:00:33,103
Biff.

892
01:00:34,644 --> 01:00:37,112
-Don't kid around. Come on.
-Come on. Leave.

893
01:00:37,235 --> 01:00:39,204
BIFF: You know you want it.

894
01:00:39,290 --> 01:00:41,242
You know you want me to give it to you.

895
01:00:41,367 --> 01:00:44,299
Shut your filthy mouth.
I'm not that kind of girl.

896
01:00:45,211 --> 01:00:47,890
Maybe you are
and you just don't know it yet.

897
01:00:48,142 --> 01:00:49,858
Get your meat hooks off of me.

898
01:00:49,986 --> 01:00:52,898
MARTY: You heard her.
She said, get your meat hooks...

899
01:00:53,615 --> 01:00:54,616
...off.

900
01:00:55,956 --> 01:00:57,119
MARTY: Please.

901
01:00:57,996 --> 01:00:59,799
What's it to you, butthead?

902
01:01:01,216 --> 01:01:03,268
BIFF: You been looking for a fight.

903
01:01:03,468 --> 01:01:04,981
[People exclaiming]

904
01:01:13,421 --> 01:01:16,515
Since you're new here,
I'm going to cut you a break...

905
01:01:17,016 --> 01:01:18,093
...today.

906
01:01:19,310 --> 01:01:21,773
So why don't you make like a tree...

907
01:01:21,987 --> 01:01:23,615
...and get out of here.

908
01:01:31,503 --> 01:01:33,018
[Boy laughing]

909
01:01:35,773 --> 01:01:36,349
MARTY: George!

910
01:01:38,440 --> 01:01:40,407
GEORGE: Why do you keep following me?

911
01:01:40,494 --> 01:01:45,300
George, I'm telling you,
if you do not ask Lorraine to that dance...

912
01:01:45,374 --> 01:01:48,381
...I'll regret it for the rest of my life.
-I can't go.

913
01:01:48,508 --> 01:01:51,688
I'll miss my favorite TV program,
Science Fiction Theatre.

914
01:01:51,773 --> 01:01:55,118
Yeah, but Lorraine wants to go with you.

915
01:01:55,317 --> 01:01:56,871
Give her a break.

916
01:01:57,197 --> 01:02:00,715
I'm just not ready
to ask Lorraine out to the dance...

917
01:02:01,379 --> 01:02:04,384
...and not you
or anybody else on this planet...

918
01:02:04,684 --> 01:02:06,899
...is going to make me change my mind.

919
01:02:14,076 --> 01:02:15,964
MARTY: Science Fiction Theatre.

920
01:02:30,539 --> 01:02:31,915
[Muffled breathing]

921
01:02:39,481 --> 01:02:41,069
[Loud rock music plays]

922
01:02:44,999 --> 01:02:46,628
[Stops music]

923
01:02:47,758 --> 01:02:48,920
Who are you?

924
01:02:49,046 --> 01:02:50,597
[Plays music]

925
01:02:53,226 --> 01:02:54,228
[Stops music]

926
01:02:54,354 --> 01:02:56,408
MARTY: [Muffled] Silence, Earthling.

927
01:02:57,859 --> 01:03:00,328
My name is Darth Vader.

928
01:03:05,386 --> 01:03:08,817
I am an extraterrestrial
from the planet Vulcan.

929
01:03:10,984 --> 01:03:11,987
Marty!

930
01:03:12,111 --> 01:03:13,738
[Car honks]

931
01:03:15,289 --> 01:03:17,506
-Marty! Marty!
-George, buddy.

932
01:03:17,582 --> 01:03:19,958
You weren't at school.
What have you been doing?

933
01:03:20,086 --> 01:03:23,016
I overslept. I need your help.

934
01:03:23,104 --> 01:03:26,108
I have to ask Lorraine out,
but I don't know how to do it.

935
01:03:26,197 --> 01:03:28,653
Keep your pants on. She's over in the cafe.

936
01:03:28,737 --> 01:03:30,791
God! How do you...?

937
01:03:33,620 --> 01:03:35,338
MARTY: What made you change your mind?

938
01:03:35,425 --> 01:03:38,354
Last night, Darth Vader came down
from planet Vulcan...

939
01:03:38,430 --> 01:03:42,362
...and told me that if I didn't
take Lorraine out, he'd melt my brain.

940
01:03:42,449 --> 01:03:45,528
Let's just keep this brain-melting stuff
to ourselves, okay?

941
01:03:45,615 --> 01:03:48,873
-Yeah. Yeah.
-All right. There she is.

942
01:03:49,210 --> 01:03:51,014
Just go in there and invite her.

943
01:03:51,089 --> 01:03:53,804
GEORGE: But I don't know what to say.
MARTY: Say anything.

944
01:03:53,893 --> 01:03:57,399
Say whatever's natural,
the first thing that comes into your mind.

945
01:03:58,563 --> 01:04:00,453
Nothing's coming to my mind.

946
01:04:00,580 --> 01:04:02,709
Jesus. It's a wonder I was even born.

947
01:04:02,835 --> 01:04:05,375
-What? What?
-Nothing.

948
01:04:06,878 --> 01:04:09,597
Tell her destiny brought you together.

949
01:04:09,683 --> 01:04:13,524
Tell her she is the most beautiful girl
you have ever seen in the world.

950
01:04:13,651 --> 01:04:15,279
Girls like that stuff.

951
01:04:15,956 --> 01:04:19,122
-What are you doing?
-I'm writing this down. This is good stuff.

952
01:04:19,248 --> 01:04:22,343
-Yeah. Okay. Can you take care of that?
-Right.

953
01:04:22,717 --> 01:04:24,520
[The Wallflower playing]

954
01:04:24,682 --> 01:04:26,474
[People chattering]

955
01:04:40,508 --> 01:04:43,276
Lou, give me a milk.

956
01:04:44,816 --> 01:04:45,906
Chocolate.

957
01:05:02,409 --> 01:05:03,660
GEORGE: Lorraine.

958
01:05:04,249 --> 01:05:05,577
My density...

959
01:05:06,252 --> 01:05:08,130
...has popped me to you.

960
01:05:08,883 --> 01:05:10,058
What?

961
01:05:10,259 --> 01:05:11,685
[Mumbling]

962
01:05:12,188 --> 01:05:14,852
What I meant to say was...

963
01:05:14,979 --> 01:05:17,735
Wait a minute.
Don't I know you from somewhere?

964
01:05:18,449 --> 01:05:19,539
GEORGE: Yes.

965
01:05:19,877 --> 01:05:23,793
Yes. I'm George. George McFly.

966
01:05:24,421 --> 01:05:26,049
I'm your density.

967
01:05:27,101 --> 01:05:28,352
I mean...

968
01:05:29,816 --> 01:05:31,068
...your destiny.

969
01:05:32,356 --> 01:05:33,737
[Sighing]

970
01:05:34,575 --> 01:05:35,501
BIFF: McFly.

971
01:05:37,417 --> 01:05:39,219
[Music stops]

972
01:05:40,097 --> 01:05:42,724
I thought I told you never to come in here.

973
01:05:45,568 --> 01:05:47,360
Well, it's going to cost you.

974
01:05:48,112 --> 01:05:50,077
How much money you got on you?

975
01:05:50,702 --> 01:05:52,503
GEORGE: How much do you want, Biff?

976
01:05:52,629 --> 01:05:53,879
[Biff grunts]

977
01:05:53,958 --> 01:05:56,012
[All gasping]

978
01:06:01,108 --> 01:06:02,521
BIFF: All right, punk.

979
01:06:02,734 --> 01:06:05,115
-Now I'm going to...
-Biff. What's that?

980
01:06:06,706 --> 01:06:07,705
[Biff grunts]

981
01:06:08,046 --> 01:06:09,673
[All gasping]

982
01:06:12,213 --> 01:06:15,307
That's Calvin Klein. My God, he's a dream.

983
01:06:16,181 --> 01:06:18,951
Kid! Kid, stop! Stop!

984
01:06:19,024 --> 01:06:20,028
[Boy protesting]

985
01:06:20,113 --> 01:06:21,706
[Dramatic instrumental music]

986
01:06:21,792 --> 01:06:23,495
MARTY: I'll get it back to you.

987
01:06:23,923 --> 01:06:25,636
BOY: You broke it!

988
01:06:25,961 --> 01:06:27,841
Come back here!

989
01:06:28,014 --> 01:06:29,429
[Cars honking]

990
01:06:29,517 --> 01:06:31,309
Look at him go!

991
01:06:32,897 --> 01:06:33,900
Get him!

992
01:06:35,077 --> 01:06:37,117
[Dramatic instrumental music]

993
01:06:47,560 --> 01:06:49,690
BIFF: To the car! Go!

994
01:06:55,999 --> 01:06:58,841
GIRL: What's that thing he's on?
BOY: A board with wheels.

995
01:06:58,930 --> 01:07:00,469
He's an absolute dream.

996
01:07:00,557 --> 01:07:03,401
THUG 1: Come on, come on!
THUG 2: Look out for the car!

997
01:07:05,943 --> 01:07:07,332
[Shouting]

998
01:07:09,499 --> 01:07:10,751
[Tires squealing]

999
01:07:17,685 --> 01:07:18,688
[Screaming]

1000
01:07:20,905 --> 01:07:22,158
[Brakes squealing]

1001
01:07:22,494 --> 01:07:23,498
[Woman grunts]

1002
01:07:25,463 --> 01:07:26,789
[Tires squealing]

1003
01:07:36,782 --> 01:07:38,246
BIFF: I'm going to ram him.

1004
01:07:46,096 --> 01:07:47,097
ALL: Shit!

1005
01:07:47,812 --> 01:07:49,064
[Brakes squealing]

1006
01:07:55,037 --> 01:07:56,915
[Crowd murmuring]

1007
01:07:58,880 --> 01:08:00,219
[Exclaiming]

1008
01:08:12,455 --> 01:08:13,881
Thanks a lot, kid.

1009
01:08:15,296 --> 01:08:17,424
I'm going to get that son of a bitch.

1010
01:08:17,814 --> 01:08:20,895
-Where does he come from?
-Yeah, where does he live?

1011
01:08:21,517 --> 01:08:22,910
I don't know...

1012
01:08:23,447 --> 01:08:25,414
...but I'm going to find out.

1013
01:08:27,165 --> 01:08:28,881
[Audio rewinding]

1014
01:08:30,971 --> 01:08:31,975
DOC ON TV: My God.

1015
01:08:32,726 --> 01:08:35,694
They found me.
I don't know how, but they found me.

1016
01:08:35,894 --> 01:08:37,445
Run for it, Marty!

1017
01:08:37,734 --> 01:08:39,536
[Audio rewinding]

1018
01:08:42,291 --> 01:08:43,294
My God.

1019
01:08:44,457 --> 01:08:47,298
They found me.
I don't know how, but they found me.

1020
01:08:47,475 --> 01:08:48,853
Run for it, Marty!

1021
01:08:50,142 --> 01:08:51,069
Doc?

1022
01:08:51,983 --> 01:08:54,950
Hi, Marty. I didn't hear you come in.

1023
01:08:55,916 --> 01:08:58,457
Fascinating device, this video unit.

1024
01:08:59,456 --> 01:09:01,088
Listen, Doc.

1025
01:09:01,209 --> 01:09:06,019
MARTY: I haven't told you about ...
DOC: One shouldn't know about his own destiny.

1026
01:09:06,107 --> 01:09:07,696
-You don't understand.
-I do.

1027
01:09:07,772 --> 01:09:10,327
If I know too much
I endanger my own existence...

1028
01:09:10,402 --> 01:09:12,793
...just as you've endangered yours.

1029
01:09:14,872 --> 01:09:16,759
You're right.

1030
01:09:16,886 --> 01:09:19,767
Let me show you my plan
for sending you home.

1031
01:09:19,844 --> 01:09:21,732
Please excuse the crudity of this model.

1032
01:09:21,858 --> 01:09:24,399
I didn't have time to build it to scale
or paint it.

1033
01:09:24,488 --> 01:09:26,616
-It's good.
-Thank you.

1034
01:09:28,457 --> 01:09:32,766
We run industrial-strength electrical cable
from the top of the clock tower...

1035
01:09:32,839 --> 01:09:36,345
...down, suspending it over the street
between these two lampposts.

1036
01:09:36,773 --> 01:09:40,779
Meanwhile, we've outfitted the time
vehicle with this big pole and hook...

1037
01:09:40,866 --> 01:09:43,958
...which runs directly
into the flux capacitor.

1038
01:09:44,872 --> 01:09:46,676
At the calculated moment...

1039
01:09:47,302 --> 01:09:49,431
...you start off from down the street...

1040
01:09:49,558 --> 01:09:53,612
...driving directly toward the cable,
accelerating to 88 miles per hour.

1041
01:09:53,862 --> 01:09:57,444
According to the flyer, at precisely
10:04 p.m. this Saturday night...

1042
01:09:57,569 --> 01:10:01,160
...lightning will strike the clock tower,
electrifying the cable...

1043
01:10:01,286 --> 01:10:03,502
...as the connecting hook makes contact...

1044
01:10:03,628 --> 01:10:07,223
...thereby sending 1.21 gigawatts
into the flux capacitor...

1045
01:10:07,646 --> 01:10:09,688
...and sending you back to 1985.

1046
01:10:09,903 --> 01:10:13,496
DOC: All right, now. Watch this.
You wind up the car and release it.

1047
01:10:15,749 --> 01:10:17,717
I'll simulate the lightning.

1048
01:10:17,877 --> 01:10:19,680
[Wind-up mechanism twisting]

1049
01:10:21,306 --> 01:10:23,185
[Electrical humming]

1050
01:10:26,281 --> 01:10:27,194
Ready.

1051
01:10:37,223 --> 01:10:38,311
Set.

1052
01:10:43,409 --> 01:10:44,411
Release.

1053
01:10:44,659 --> 01:10:46,451
[Car whirring]

1054
01:10:48,542 --> 01:10:49,719
[Marty exclaims]

1055
01:10:56,779 --> 01:10:58,034
[Gasps]

1056
01:11:02,956 --> 01:11:05,345
MARTY: You're instilling me
with a lot of confidence.

1057
01:11:05,422 --> 01:11:09,103
Don't worry. I'll take care of ideas.
You take care of your pop.

1058
01:11:10,316 --> 01:11:11,317
By the way...

1059
01:11:11,443 --> 01:11:13,662
...what happened today? Did he ask her out?

1060
01:11:13,735 --> 01:11:15,789
-I think so.
-What did she say?

1061
01:11:16,201 --> 01:11:17,628
[Knocking at door]

1062
01:11:24,429 --> 01:11:28,734
It's your mom! She's tracked you down!
Quick! Let's cover the time machine.

1063
01:11:42,057 --> 01:11:44,524
Hi, Cal... Marty.

1064
01:11:45,651 --> 01:11:46,740
MARTY: Lorraine.

1065
01:11:47,990 --> 01:11:49,872
How did you know I was here?

1066
01:11:51,135 --> 01:11:52,387
I followed you.

1067
01:11:54,142 --> 01:11:56,267
This is my doc... My uncle...

1068
01:11:57,108 --> 01:11:59,074
...Doc Brown.

1069
01:12:00,025 --> 01:12:01,277
-Hi.
-Hi.

1070
01:12:04,369 --> 01:12:07,712
LORRAINE:
Marty, this may seem a little forward...

1071
01:12:07,925 --> 01:12:10,142
...but I was kind of wondering...

1072
01:12:10,842 --> 01:12:12,985
...if you'd ask me to the...

1073
01:12:13,773 --> 01:12:16,776
...Enchantment Under the Sea dance
on Saturday?

1074
01:12:18,745 --> 01:12:19,919
You mean...

1075
01:12:20,335 --> 01:12:22,212
You mean nobody's asked you?

1076
01:12:22,625 --> 01:12:23,554
LORRAINE: No.

1077
01:12:24,716 --> 01:12:26,058
Not yet.

1078
01:12:27,434 --> 01:12:30,775
-What about George?
-George McFly?

1079
01:12:33,406 --> 01:12:37,424
He's kind of cute and all, but not...

1080
01:12:38,302 --> 01:12:39,306
Well...

1081
01:12:40,179 --> 01:12:42,396
I think a man should be strong...

1082
01:12:44,350 --> 01:12:46,566
...so he can stand up for himself...

1083
01:12:47,653 --> 01:12:50,034
...and protect the woman he loves.

1084
01:12:54,632 --> 01:12:55,719
Don't you?

1085
01:12:57,523 --> 01:12:58,350
Yeah.

1086
01:12:59,438 --> 01:13:01,314
I still don't understand.

1087
01:13:01,531 --> 01:13:04,243
How am I supposed to
go to the dance with her...

1088
01:13:04,318 --> 01:13:07,089
...if she's already going
to the dance with you?

1089
01:13:07,163 --> 01:13:09,843
Well, because, George,
she wants to go with you.

1090
01:13:09,931 --> 01:13:11,808
She just doesn't know it yet.

1091
01:13:11,936 --> 01:13:14,937
That's why we got to show her
that you, George, are a fighter.

1092
01:13:15,063 --> 01:13:18,494
You're somebody who stands up
for yourself and will protect her.

1093
01:13:18,620 --> 01:13:21,702
Yeah, but I've never picked a fight
in my entire life.

1094
01:13:21,911 --> 01:13:24,668
Look, you're not going
to be picking a fight, Dad...

1095
01:13:24,757 --> 01:13:26,468
Dad-dad-daddy-o.

1096
01:13:27,058 --> 01:13:29,263
You're coming to her rescue, right?

1097
01:13:29,440 --> 01:13:32,444
Let's go over the plan again.
8:55, where are you going to be?

1098
01:13:32,533 --> 01:13:34,107
I'm going to be at the dance.

1099
01:13:34,196 --> 01:13:37,288
-And where am I going to be?
-You'll be in the car, with her.

1100
01:13:37,415 --> 01:13:40,380
Right around 9:00,
she's going to get very angry with me.

1101
01:13:40,472 --> 01:13:42,848
Why is she going to get angry with you?

1102
01:13:42,973 --> 01:13:47,530
Well, because, George, nice girls get angry
when guys take advantage of them.

1103
01:13:48,407 --> 01:13:49,411
[George exclaims]

1104
01:13:49,911 --> 01:13:54,457
-You're going to go touch her on her...
-No. No, George, look.

1105
01:13:55,294 --> 01:13:57,260
It's just an act, right?

1106
01:13:57,550 --> 01:14:01,317
So, 9:00, you're strolling
through the parking lot.

1107
01:14:01,480 --> 01:14:04,910
You see us struggling in the car.

1108
01:14:05,110 --> 01:14:08,291
You walk up, you open the door,
and you say...

1109
01:14:09,922 --> 01:14:11,510
Your line, George.

1110
01:14:12,887 --> 01:14:14,226
[Exclaiming]

1111
01:14:14,802 --> 01:14:17,645
Hey, you, get your damn hands off her!

1112
01:14:19,983 --> 01:14:22,365
-You really think I ought to swear?
-Definitely.

1113
01:14:22,492 --> 01:14:24,116
Goddamn it, George, swear.

1114
01:14:24,205 --> 01:14:27,137
Okay. So now you come up.
You punch me in the stomach.

1115
01:14:27,212 --> 01:14:29,102
I'm out for the count, right?

1116
01:14:29,391 --> 01:14:32,481
And you and Lorraine
live happily ever after.

1117
01:14:32,681 --> 01:14:34,823
You make it sound so easy.

1118
01:14:34,950 --> 01:14:37,741
I just... I wish I wasn't so scared.

1119
01:14:37,830 --> 01:14:41,421
There's nothing to be scared of.
All it takes is self-confidence.

1120
01:14:41,496 --> 01:14:43,889
You know, if you put your mind to it...

1121
01:14:44,264 --> 01:14:46,304
...you can accomplish anything.

1122
01:14:48,022 --> 01:14:50,574
ANNOUNCER:
Hill Valley weather this Saturday night.

1123
01:14:50,699 --> 01:14:53,532
Mostly clear, with some scattered clouds.

1124
01:14:53,617 --> 01:14:55,494
Lows tonight, in the upper forties.

1125
01:14:55,584 --> 01:14:58,252
Are you sure about this storm?

1126
01:14:58,338 --> 01:15:02,647
Since when can weathermen predict
the weather, let alone the future?

1127
01:15:02,732 --> 01:15:05,437
You know, Marty,
I'm going to be very sad to see you go.

1128
01:15:05,527 --> 01:15:09,206
You've made a difference in my life,
given me something to shoot for.

1129
01:15:09,279 --> 01:15:13,465
Just knowing
that I'm going to be around to see 1985.

1130
01:15:13,551 --> 01:15:15,430
That I'm going to succeed in this!

1131
01:15:15,555 --> 01:15:18,559
That I'm going to have a chance
to travel through time!

1132
01:15:19,021 --> 01:15:21,153
[Emotional instrumental music]

1133
01:15:24,581 --> 01:15:27,249
It's going to be hard
waiting 30 years to talk to you...

1134
01:15:27,338 --> 01:15:30,304
...about everything that's happened
in the past few days.

1135
01:15:30,379 --> 01:15:32,771
I'm really going to miss you, Marty.

1136
01:15:34,311 --> 01:15:35,814
I'm going to miss you.

1137
01:15:40,533 --> 01:15:43,466
-Doc, about the future...
-No!

1138
01:15:43,541 --> 01:15:48,772
We've agreed that information about
the future can be extremely dangerous.

1139
01:15:48,899 --> 01:15:52,820
Even if your intentions are good,
it can backfire drastically.

1140
01:15:53,691 --> 01:15:58,089
Whatever you've got to tell me I'll find out
through the natural course of time.

1141
01:15:59,128 --> 01:16:01,095
"Dear Dr. Brown:

1142
01:16:01,845 --> 01:16:04,686
"On the night that I go back in time...

1143
01:16:04,762 --> 01:16:06,980
"...you will be...

1144
01:16:07,106 --> 01:16:10,447
"...shot by terrorists.

1145
01:16:10,535 --> 01:16:15,092
"Please take whatever precautions are
necessary to prevent this terrible disaster.

1146
01:16:16,972 --> 01:16:18,685
"Your friend...

1147
01:16:19,511 --> 01:16:21,013
"...Marty."

1148
01:16:30,419 --> 01:16:33,350
'Evening, Dr. Brown. What's with the wire?

1149
01:16:33,476 --> 01:16:36,389
Just a little weather experiment.

1150
01:16:37,896 --> 01:16:40,285
-What you got under here?
-No! Don't touch that!

1151
01:16:40,412 --> 01:16:43,203
Some new, specialized
weather-sensing equipment.

1152
01:16:43,290 --> 01:16:45,506
Got a permit for that?

1153
01:16:45,881 --> 01:16:47,511
Of course I do.

1154
01:16:54,359 --> 01:16:57,200
DOC: Just a second.
Let me see if I can find it here.

1155
01:16:59,043 --> 01:17:00,832
[Playing rock 'n' roll music]

1156
01:17:34,840 --> 01:17:36,893
[Music continues faintly]

1157
01:17:46,835 --> 01:17:48,339
[Marty sighs]

1158
01:17:49,714 --> 01:17:51,930
Do you mind if we...

1159
01:17:52,021 --> 01:17:54,560
...park for a while?

1160
01:17:55,525 --> 01:17:58,618
That's a great idea. I'd love to park.

1161
01:17:58,704 --> 01:18:03,427
I'm almost 18 years old.
It's not like I've never parked before.

1162
01:18:04,213 --> 01:18:05,717
What?

1163
01:18:06,056 --> 01:18:09,024
Marty, you seem so nervous.
Is something wrong?

1164
01:18:09,148 --> 01:18:10,224
No.

1165
01:18:11,315 --> 01:18:12,405
No.

1166
01:18:12,941 --> 01:18:14,658
[Opening bottle]

1167
01:18:17,085 --> 01:18:19,464
Lorraine, what are you doing?

1168
01:18:20,092 --> 01:18:22,381
I swiped it from
the old lady's liquor cabinet.

1169
01:18:22,507 --> 01:18:25,310
Yeah, well, you shouldn't drink.

1170
01:18:25,524 --> 01:18:27,402
-Why not?
-Because you...

1171
01:18:28,155 --> 01:18:31,747
You might regret it later in life.

1172
01:18:31,837 --> 01:18:33,877
Marty, don't be such a square.

1173
01:18:34,004 --> 01:18:36,758
Everybody who's anybody drinks.

1174
01:18:41,275 --> 01:18:43,065
Jeez, you smoke too?

1175
01:18:44,494 --> 01:18:47,411
You're beginning to sound
just like my mother.

1176
01:18:48,164 --> 01:18:49,754
[Jazz song ending]

1177
01:18:53,560 --> 01:18:55,512
[Crowd applauding]

1178
01:18:58,729 --> 01:19:02,326
We're going to take a little break,
but we'll be back in a while...

1179
01:19:02,413 --> 01:19:05,092
...so don't nobody go nowhere.

1180
01:19:20,793 --> 01:19:24,050
Marty? Why are you so nervous?

1181
01:19:25,352 --> 01:19:26,604
Lorraine...

1182
01:19:27,645 --> 01:19:29,521
...have you ever...

1183
01:19:30,611 --> 01:19:34,541
...been in a situation where you knew
you had to act a certain way, but when...

1184
01:19:34,630 --> 01:19:37,797
...you got there, you didn't know
if you could go through with it?

1185
01:19:37,884 --> 01:19:40,975
You mean like how
you're supposed to act on a first date?

1186
01:19:41,429 --> 01:19:43,020
Sort of.

1187
01:19:43,107 --> 01:19:46,363
I think I know exactly what you mean.

1188
01:19:46,989 --> 01:19:48,238
You do?

1189
01:19:48,791 --> 01:19:51,419
You know what I do in those situations?

1190
01:19:51,507 --> 01:19:53,801
-What?
-I don't worry.

1191
01:19:54,676 --> 01:19:56,390
[Marty protesting muffled]

1192
01:20:11,640 --> 01:20:13,522
LORRAINE: This is all wrong.

1193
01:20:13,645 --> 01:20:15,951
I don't know what it is...

1194
01:20:17,114 --> 01:20:20,794
...but when I kiss you, it's like I'm kissing...

1195
01:20:22,371 --> 01:20:23,749
...my brother.

1196
01:20:24,051 --> 01:20:26,767
I guess that doesn't make any sense,
does it?

1197
01:20:26,843 --> 01:20:28,483
Believe me...

1198
01:20:29,306 --> 01:20:31,023
...it makes perfect sense.

1199
01:20:31,275 --> 01:20:32,276
[Footsteps approaching]

1200
01:20:32,401 --> 01:20:33,331
Somebody's coming.

1201
01:20:35,080 --> 01:20:36,333
[Marty grunts]

1202
01:20:38,248 --> 01:20:42,394
You caused $300 damage to my car,
you son of a bitch...

1203
01:20:42,644 --> 01:20:44,936
...and I'm going to take it out of your ass.

1204
01:20:45,060 --> 01:20:46,612
Hold him.

1205
01:20:46,690 --> 01:20:49,457
Let him go, Biff. You're drunk.

1206
01:20:50,119 --> 01:20:52,334
Well, lookee what we have here.

1207
01:20:55,378 --> 01:20:58,971
No! Stay right here with me.
Come on, Lorraine.

1208
01:20:59,096 --> 01:21:01,313
LORRAINE: Let me go!
MARTY: Leave her alone!

1209
01:21:01,401 --> 01:21:03,366
Take him in back. I'll be right there.

1210
01:21:03,490 --> 01:21:06,495
Just admit that you want it. Come on.

1211
01:21:06,660 --> 01:21:09,214
Well, go on. This ain't no peep show.

1212
01:21:09,465 --> 01:21:10,840
[Marty grunts]

1213
01:21:11,305 --> 01:21:13,096
[Lorraine and Biff struggling]

1214
01:21:13,557 --> 01:21:15,349
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

1215
01:21:15,725 --> 01:21:18,029
THUG 1: Hey, let's put him in there.
THUG 2: Yeah!

1216
01:21:19,744 --> 01:21:22,034
That's for messing up my hair.

1217
01:21:22,159 --> 01:21:24,215
What the hell are you doing to my car?

1218
01:21:24,341 --> 01:21:27,258
Hey, beat it, spook.
This don't concern you.

1219
01:21:29,385 --> 01:21:32,403
Who you calling spook, peckerwood?

1220
01:21:33,445 --> 01:21:35,033
THUG 2: Listen, guys...

1221
01:21:35,119 --> 01:21:37,787
...I don't want to mess
with no reefer addicts, okay?

1222
01:21:37,875 --> 01:21:39,664
MARVIN: Get home to your mama, boy.

1223
01:21:39,790 --> 01:21:42,006
THUG 1: Biff!
THUG 2: Hurry, Biff!

1224
01:21:42,595 --> 01:21:44,562
MARTY: [Muffled] Let me out of here!

1225
01:21:44,687 --> 01:21:46,138
[Pounding and shouting]

1226
01:21:46,227 --> 01:21:47,855
Reginald, where are your keys?

1227
01:21:47,941 --> 01:21:50,157
MARTY: [Muffled] The keys are in the trunk.

1228
01:21:50,446 --> 01:21:53,449
MARVIN: Say that again?
MARTY: I said, the keys are in here.

1229
01:21:58,295 --> 01:21:59,936
LORRAINE: [Muffled] Let me go!

1230
01:22:07,236 --> 01:22:08,324
BIFF: [Muffled] Come on!

1231
01:22:10,628 --> 01:22:14,137
Hey you, get your damn hands off...

1232
01:22:14,547 --> 01:22:16,016
[Sighing]

1233
01:22:16,602 --> 01:22:18,818
I think you got the wrong car, McFly.

1234
01:22:20,522 --> 01:22:22,574
George, help me! Please!

1235
01:22:22,660 --> 01:22:26,257
Just turn around, McFly, and walk away.

1236
01:22:26,581 --> 01:22:28,710
LORRAINE: Please, George.
BIFF: Are you deaf?

1237
01:22:28,834 --> 01:22:30,890
BIFF: Close the door and beat it.

1238
01:22:31,101 --> 01:22:32,517
[Lorraine whimpering]

1239
01:22:33,520 --> 01:22:37,198
No, Biff. You leave her alone.

1240
01:22:37,865 --> 01:22:39,667
All right, McFly.

1241
01:22:42,922 --> 01:22:44,888
You're asking for it...

1242
01:22:45,088 --> 01:22:47,142
...and now you're going to get it.

1243
01:22:47,266 --> 01:22:48,270
[George groaning]

1244
01:22:48,394 --> 01:22:49,643
Stop it!

1245
01:22:50,485 --> 01:22:52,364
Biff, you'll break his arm!

1246
01:22:52,525 --> 01:22:53,954
[Gasping]

1247
01:22:54,241 --> 01:22:56,119
LORRAINE: Biff, no!

1248
01:22:56,331 --> 01:22:58,209
MARVIN: Give me a hand here, Reginald.

1249
01:22:58,839 --> 01:22:59,837
[Exclaiming]

1250
01:22:59,962 --> 01:23:02,180
Damn it, man! I sliced my hand!

1251
01:23:02,265 --> 01:23:03,808
-Whose are these?
-Mine.

1252
01:23:03,895 --> 01:23:05,773
Thanks. Thanks a lot.

1253
01:23:07,150 --> 01:23:09,453
You're going to break his arm!

1254
01:23:09,703 --> 01:23:12,960
Biff! Biff, leave him alone!

1255
01:23:13,048 --> 01:23:15,514
Let him go! Let him go!

1256
01:23:17,554 --> 01:23:19,184
[Biff laughing]

1257
01:23:20,399 --> 01:23:22,691
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

1258
01:23:30,051 --> 01:23:31,053
[Biff grunts]

1259
01:23:38,778 --> 01:23:40,331
[George panting]

1260
01:23:48,518 --> 01:23:50,398
Are you okay?

1261
01:23:54,279 --> 01:23:56,659
[Tender instrumental music]

1262
01:24:10,283 --> 01:24:12,248
Who is that guy?

1263
01:24:12,571 --> 01:24:14,461
That's George McFly.

1264
01:24:17,757 --> 01:24:19,560
That's George McFly?

1265
01:24:19,687 --> 01:24:21,136
Excuse me.

1266
01:24:23,528 --> 01:24:25,321
[Thunder rumbling]

1267
01:24:32,469 --> 01:24:33,972
The storm.

1268
01:24:35,938 --> 01:24:38,655
Guys, you got to get back in there
and finish the dance.

1269
01:24:38,729 --> 01:24:40,159
Look at Marvin's hand.

1270
01:24:40,232 --> 01:24:43,500
He can't play with this hand,
and we can't play without him.

1271
01:24:43,626 --> 01:24:45,128
Marvin, you got to play.

1272
01:24:45,203 --> 01:24:49,135
They kiss for the first time at the dance.
If there's no music, they can't dance.

1273
01:24:49,221 --> 01:24:52,978
If they can't dance and kiss,
then they can't fall in love and I'm history.

1274
01:24:53,103 --> 01:24:57,036
The dance is over unless you know
somebody that can play the guitar.

1275
01:24:57,162 --> 01:24:59,203
[Playing Earth Angel]

1276
01:25:04,299 --> 01:25:06,677
This is for all you lovers out there.

1277
01:25:09,194 --> 01:25:12,787
MARVIN: [Singing]
"Earth Angel, Earth Angel

1278
01:25:13,864 --> 01:25:16,081
"Will you be mine

1279
01:25:17,255 --> 01:25:19,636
"My darling dear"

1280
01:25:20,801 --> 01:25:22,928
LORRAINE:
George, aren't you going to kiss me?

1281
01:25:23,017 --> 01:25:24,983
I don't know.

1282
01:25:26,024 --> 01:25:28,490
Scram, McFly. I'm cutting in.

1283
01:25:28,664 --> 01:25:30,366
[Lorraine protesting]

1284
01:25:32,921 --> 01:25:35,050
[Playing dissonant notes]

1285
01:25:35,175 --> 01:25:38,266
"Earth Angel, Earth Angel

1286
01:25:39,396 --> 01:25:41,611
"The one I adore"

1287
01:25:41,736 --> 01:25:43,528
Hey, boy, you all right?

1288
01:25:44,867 --> 01:25:46,331
I can't play.

1289
01:25:46,457 --> 01:25:47,708
George!

1290
01:25:48,085 --> 01:25:49,889
[Boy laughing]

1291
01:25:50,299 --> 01:25:52,677
[Oppressive instrumental music]

1292
01:25:56,937 --> 01:25:59,077
[Ominous instrumental music]

1293
01:26:04,175 --> 01:26:05,174
[Boy laughing]

1294
01:26:05,302 --> 01:26:06,302
George!

1295
01:26:07,516 --> 01:26:08,770
George.

1296
01:26:11,898 --> 01:26:13,525
Excuse me.

1297
01:26:13,692 --> 01:26:14,954
[Boy grunts]

1298
01:26:15,830 --> 01:26:17,959
[Uplifting instrumental music]

1299
01:26:22,181 --> 01:26:25,097
MARVIN: [Singing]
"The vision of your happiness

1300
01:26:27,687 --> 01:26:30,693
"Earth Angel, Earth Angel

1301
01:26:31,494 --> 01:26:33,875
"Please be mine

1302
01:26:35,163 --> 01:26:37,217
"My darling dear

1303
01:26:37,555 --> 01:26:39,595
"Love you for all time

1304
01:26:41,399 --> 01:26:43,779
"I'm just a fool

1305
01:26:45,820 --> 01:26:48,210
"A fool in love

1306
01:26:50,252 --> 01:26:53,343
"with you"

1307
01:26:58,063 --> 01:26:59,779
[Crowd applauding]

1308
01:27:00,493 --> 01:27:04,325
MARVIN: Yeah, man, that was good.
Let's do another one.

1309
01:27:04,452 --> 01:27:06,416
No. I got to go.

1310
01:27:06,503 --> 01:27:09,596
Come on, man.
Let's do something that really cooks.

1311
01:27:10,096 --> 01:27:13,193
Something that cooks.

1312
01:27:13,478 --> 01:27:15,107
[Cheering]

1313
01:27:18,286 --> 01:27:20,416
MARTY: All right.
MARVIN: All right!

1314
01:27:24,422 --> 01:27:25,811
All right.

1315
01:27:26,725 --> 01:27:30,155
All right, this is an oldie, but...

1316
01:27:32,161 --> 01:27:35,918
Well, it's an oldie where I come from.

1317
01:27:37,540 --> 01:27:39,849
All right, listen, this is a blues riff in B.

1318
01:27:39,924 --> 01:27:42,938
Watch me for the changes,
and try and keep up, okay?

1319
01:27:43,316 --> 01:27:45,107
[Playing Johnny B. Goode]

1320
01:27:59,945 --> 01:28:02,951
"Way down in Louisiana
Down in New Orleans

1321
01:28:03,035 --> 01:28:06,040
"Way back up in the woods
among the evergreens

1322
01:28:06,166 --> 01:28:08,834
"There stood a log cabin
made of earth and wood

1323
01:28:08,922 --> 01:28:11,550
"Where lived a country boy
name of Johnny B. Goode

1324
01:28:11,640 --> 01:28:14,019
"He never ever learned
to read or write so well

1325
01:28:14,105 --> 01:28:16,858
"He could play the guitar
just like he's ringin' a bell

1326
01:28:16,949 --> 01:28:20,955
"Go go
Go Johnny go, go

1327
01:28:21,417 --> 01:28:24,047
"Go Johnny, go, go, go

1328
01:28:24,336 --> 01:28:26,050
"Go Johnny go, go"

1329
01:28:26,138 --> 01:28:28,517
George, I heard you laid out Biff.
Nice going.

1330
01:28:28,605 --> 01:28:30,896
You ever think of
running for class president?

1331
01:28:30,983 --> 01:28:33,364
MARTY: [Singing] "Johnny B. Goode

1332
01:28:35,742 --> 01:28:38,123
"Go Johnny go, go

1333
01:28:38,495 --> 01:28:41,175
"Go Johnny go, go, go

1334
01:28:41,265 --> 01:28:43,969
"Go Johnny go, go

1335
01:28:44,055 --> 01:28:46,934
"Go Johnny go, go, go

1336
01:28:47,611 --> 01:28:49,577
"Johnny B. Goode"

1337
01:28:52,031 --> 01:28:54,837
Chuck! Chuck, it's Marvin.

1338
01:28:54,925 --> 01:28:57,465
Your cousin, Marvin Berry?

1339
01:28:57,593 --> 01:29:00,346
You know that new sound
you're looking for?

1340
01:29:00,435 --> 01:29:02,561
Well, listen to this.

1341
01:29:24,749 --> 01:29:26,629
[Music growing wilder]

1342
01:29:33,153 --> 01:29:35,443
[Marty playing heavy metal riffs]

1343
01:29:48,977 --> 01:29:50,155
[Feedback]

1344
01:29:53,912 --> 01:29:55,966
[Playing sustained high-pitch note]

1345
01:29:56,502 --> 01:29:58,379
[Amplifiers humming]

1346
01:30:11,955 --> 01:30:14,508
I guess you guys aren't ready for that yet...

1347
01:30:15,972 --> 01:30:17,938
...but your kids are going to love it.

1348
01:30:18,390 --> 01:30:20,442
[Jazz playing faintly]

1349
01:30:22,158 --> 01:30:23,408
Lorraine.

1350
01:30:23,573 --> 01:30:26,251
Marty, that was very interesting music.

1351
01:30:27,004 --> 01:30:28,257
Yeah.

1352
01:30:28,382 --> 01:30:31,974
I hope you don't mind, but George
asked if he could take me home.

1353
01:30:32,048 --> 01:30:36,695
Great! Good. Good, Lorraine.
I had a feeling about you two.

1354
01:30:37,110 --> 01:30:38,700
I have a feeling, too.

1355
01:30:39,451 --> 01:30:41,330
Listen, I got to go, but...

1356
01:30:41,455 --> 01:30:45,675
...I wanted to tell you that
it's been educational.

1357
01:30:46,727 --> 01:30:48,928
LORRAINE: Marty, will we ever see you again?

1358
01:30:50,392 --> 01:30:51,569
I guarantee it.

1359
01:30:51,695 --> 01:30:55,037
Well, Marty, I want to thank you
for all your good advice.

1360
01:30:55,165 --> 01:30:57,619
I'll never forget it.

1361
01:30:58,258 --> 01:30:59,794
Right, George.

1362
01:31:01,213 --> 01:31:04,730
Well, good luck, you guys.

1363
01:31:06,184 --> 01:31:07,524
[Marty exclaims]

1364
01:31:08,700 --> 01:31:10,491
One other thing.

1365
01:31:10,617 --> 01:31:12,581
If you guys ever have kids...

1366
01:31:12,706 --> 01:31:15,084
...and one of them,
when he's eight years old...

1367
01:31:15,210 --> 01:31:18,215
...accidentally sets fire
to the living room rug...

1368
01:31:19,605 --> 01:31:21,183
...go easy on him.

1369
01:31:22,184 --> 01:31:23,450
Okay.

1370
01:31:28,369 --> 01:31:31,639
Marty. Such a nice name.

1371
01:31:37,183 --> 01:31:39,063
Damn! Where is that kid?

1372
01:31:40,078 --> 01:31:42,457
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

1373
01:31:42,534 --> 01:31:43,784
Damn!

1374
01:31:46,249 --> 01:31:47,678
Damn! Damn!

1375
01:31:50,724 --> 01:31:52,310
[Brakes squealing]

1376
01:31:55,490 --> 01:31:58,370
DOC: You're late!
Do you have no concept of time?

1377
01:31:58,459 --> 01:32:00,839
Hey, come on. I had to change.

1378
01:32:00,925 --> 01:32:03,806
Do you think I'm going back
in that zoot suit?

1379
01:32:03,928 --> 01:32:06,561
The old man really came through.
It worked.

1380
01:32:06,686 --> 01:32:08,728
He laid out Biff in one punch.

1381
01:32:08,851 --> 01:32:12,907
I didn't know he had it in him.
He's never stood up to Biff in his life.

1382
01:32:14,623 --> 01:32:18,053
-Never?
-No. Why? What's the matter?

1383
01:32:18,716 --> 01:32:19,968
All right!

1384
01:32:20,259 --> 01:32:22,397
Let's set your destination time.

1385
01:32:25,353 --> 01:32:26,870
[Machine beeping]

1386
01:32:27,195 --> 01:32:29,498
DOC: This is the exact time you left.

1387
01:32:29,947 --> 01:32:33,377
Let's send you back at exactly
the same time.

1388
01:32:36,311 --> 01:32:38,352
It'll be like you never left.

1389
01:32:40,230 --> 01:32:44,538
Now, I painted a white line
on the street way over there.

1390
01:32:44,750 --> 01:32:46,628
That's where you'll start from.

1391
01:32:46,714 --> 01:32:49,006
I've calculated the precise distance...

1392
01:32:49,096 --> 01:32:51,811
...taking into account
the acceleration speed...

1393
01:32:51,884 --> 01:32:56,157
...and wind resistance retroactive
from the moment the lightning strikes...

1394
01:32:56,443 --> 01:33:01,077
...which will be in exactly
7 minutes and 22 seconds.

1395
01:33:01,329 --> 01:33:03,718
When this alarm goes off, you hit the gas.

1396
01:33:03,847 --> 01:33:05,096
Right.

1397
01:33:05,547 --> 01:33:07,851
[Dramatic instrumental music]

1398
01:33:11,692 --> 01:33:14,862
Well, I guess that's everything.

1399
01:33:18,954 --> 01:33:21,347
-Thanks.
-Thank you.

1400
01:33:26,770 --> 01:33:28,736
DOC: See you in about 30 years.

1401
01:33:30,576 --> 01:33:32,291
I hope so.

1402
01:33:33,583 --> 01:33:35,209
Don't worry!

1403
01:33:35,836 --> 01:33:37,550
As long as you hit that wire...

1404
01:33:37,636 --> 01:33:40,730
...with the connecting hook
at precisely 88 miles an hour...

1405
01:33:40,806 --> 01:33:43,774
...the instant the lightning
strikes the tower...

1406
01:33:46,241 --> 01:33:48,457
DOC: ...everything will be fine.
MARTY: Right.

1407
01:33:55,804 --> 01:33:57,433
[Thunder rumbling]

1408
01:33:57,559 --> 01:34:01,240
-What's the meaning of this?
-You'll find out in 30 years.

1409
01:34:01,330 --> 01:34:03,368
It's about the future, isn't it?

1410
01:34:03,493 --> 01:34:06,211
DOC: Information about the future.
MARTY: Wait a minute.

1411
01:34:06,300 --> 01:34:08,266
I warned you about this, kid.

1412
01:34:08,340 --> 01:34:11,144
The consequences could be disastrous!

1413
01:34:11,270 --> 01:34:13,649
Doc, that's a risk
you're going to have to take.

1414
01:34:13,775 --> 01:34:16,954
-Your life depends on it!
-No!

1415
01:34:17,080 --> 01:34:20,422
I refuse to accept the responsibility.

1416
01:34:20,711 --> 01:34:23,391
In that case, I'll tell you straight out.

1417
01:34:24,517 --> 01:34:26,648
[Thunder crashing]

1418
01:34:27,484 --> 01:34:28,862
Great Scott!

1419
01:34:31,779 --> 01:34:33,494
[Thunder clapping]

1420
01:34:36,838 --> 01:34:39,844
You get the cable,
I'll throw the rope down to you.

1421
01:34:39,968 --> 01:34:42,099
MARTY: Right! I got it!

1422
01:35:10,257 --> 01:35:11,971
[Wind howling]

1423
01:35:13,399 --> 01:35:14,725
[Gasping]

1424
01:35:15,978 --> 01:35:17,243
Doc!

1425
01:35:24,918 --> 01:35:29,014
DOC: Come on, come on. Let's go!
MARTY: All right! Take it up!

1426
01:35:32,482 --> 01:35:33,571
Go!

1427
01:35:40,670 --> 01:35:41,674
Doc!

1428
01:35:41,799 --> 01:35:42,811
[Shouting indistinctly]

1429
01:35:42,937 --> 01:35:46,445
I have to tell you about the future.

1430
01:35:46,532 --> 01:35:50,826
DOC: What?
MARTY: I have to tell you about the future!

1431
01:35:51,002 --> 01:35:52,328
What?

1432
01:35:52,416 --> 01:35:57,136
On the night I go back in time you get...

1433
01:35:57,422 --> 01:35:58,977
[Clock bells ringing]

1434
01:36:00,765 --> 01:36:02,109
Doc!

1435
01:36:07,702 --> 01:36:09,295
[Doc screaming]

1436
01:36:11,385 --> 01:36:12,299
Go!

1437
01:36:13,351 --> 01:36:15,568
-No, Doc!
-Look at the time!

1438
01:36:15,894 --> 01:36:18,021
You've got less than four minutes.

1439
01:36:18,146 --> 01:36:19,861
Please, hurry!

1440
01:36:19,950 --> 01:36:21,539
[Dramatic instrumental music]

1441
01:36:21,615 --> 01:36:23,206
[Bell ringing]

1442
01:36:31,518 --> 01:36:32,858
DOC: Yeah!

1443
01:37:26,162 --> 01:37:29,427
Damn it, Doc!
Why did you have to tear up that letter?

1444
01:37:29,553 --> 01:37:31,522
If I only had more time.

1445
01:37:34,526 --> 01:37:36,904
Wait a minute. I got all the time I want.

1446
01:37:36,994 --> 01:37:40,584
I got a time machine.
I can just go back early and warn him.

1447
01:37:40,662 --> 01:37:42,790
Okay. Ten minutes ought to do it.

1448
01:37:44,755 --> 01:37:47,059
Okay. Time circuit's on.

1449
01:37:47,398 --> 01:37:50,064
Flux capacitor, fluxing.

1450
01:37:50,529 --> 01:37:52,567
Engine running. All right.

1451
01:37:52,744 --> 01:37:54,696
[Engine stops suddenly]

1452
01:37:55,446 --> 01:37:57,839
No. No, no.

1453
01:37:57,965 --> 01:38:01,308
Come on. Come on. Not this time.

1454
01:38:21,316 --> 01:38:22,819
[Doc screaming]

1455
01:38:28,578 --> 01:38:30,469
[Engine clicking]

1456
01:38:35,691 --> 01:38:36,693
[Grunts]

1457
01:39:11,828 --> 01:39:13,079
MARTY: Come on.

1458
01:39:13,204 --> 01:39:14,833
[Alarm ringing]

1459
01:39:15,209 --> 01:39:17,965
Here we go. Here we go.
This time. Come on.

1460
01:39:19,638 --> 01:39:22,521
Please. Please. Come on!

1461
01:39:22,932 --> 01:39:23,947
[Shouts]

1462
01:39:24,021 --> 01:39:25,026
[Engine starts]

1463
01:39:28,204 --> 01:39:29,457
[Tires squealing]

1464
01:39:43,530 --> 01:39:44,545
[Grunts]

1465
01:40:05,555 --> 01:40:07,184
[Screaming]

1466
01:40:18,878 --> 01:40:21,182
[Dramatic instrumental music]

1467
01:40:48,501 --> 01:40:50,093
[Screaming]

1468
01:41:01,037 --> 01:41:02,413
Doc.

1469
01:41:20,844 --> 01:41:23,136
[Wind blowing]

1470
01:41:50,794 --> 01:41:52,837
[Doc shouting gleefully]

1471
01:42:03,166 --> 01:42:04,793
[Helicopter whirring]

1472
01:42:18,404 --> 01:42:20,206
[Music playing on radio]

1473
01:42:23,336 --> 01:42:24,763
[Explosive crashing]

1474
01:42:26,932 --> 01:42:28,557
[Tires squealing]

1475
01:42:38,750 --> 01:42:40,802
Crazy drunk driver.

1476
01:42:51,332 --> 01:42:52,677
[Marty exclaims]

1477
01:42:55,840 --> 01:42:57,895
All right. Fred.

1478
01:42:59,774 --> 01:43:02,491
You look great. Everything looks great.

1479
01:43:02,567 --> 01:43:03,742
[Marty panting]

1480
01:43:03,869 --> 01:43:04,869
1:24.

1481
01:43:04,994 --> 01:43:07,874
I still got time. I'm coming, Doc!

1482
01:43:09,627 --> 01:43:10,968
[Engine dies]

1483
01:43:11,505 --> 01:43:13,898
No! No, not again!

1484
01:43:13,974 --> 01:43:16,439
Come on! Come on!

1485
01:43:16,565 --> 01:43:18,366
[Tires squealing]

1486
01:43:21,962 --> 01:43:23,588
[Terrorists shouting indistinctly]

1487
01:43:30,098 --> 01:43:31,478
Libyans.

1488
01:43:35,368 --> 01:43:37,324
[Suspenseful instrumental music]

1489
01:43:54,079 --> 01:43:55,166
MARTY: No!

1490
01:43:56,508 --> 01:43:57,847
Bastards!

1491
01:44:11,344 --> 01:44:12,672
TERRORIST: Go!

1492
01:44:13,674 --> 01:44:15,264
[Tires squealing]

1493
01:44:17,193 --> 01:44:18,695
[Gunshots firing]

1494
01:44:47,142 --> 01:44:49,270
MARTY: Doc! Doc!

1495
01:44:49,733 --> 01:44:51,362
[Marty sobbing]

1496
01:44:51,486 --> 01:44:53,703
[Tender instrumental music]

1497
01:44:56,295 --> 01:44:57,209
MARTY: No!

1498
01:45:11,873 --> 01:45:13,336
You're alive.

1499
01:45:22,616 --> 01:45:24,157
Bulletproof vest?

1500
01:45:25,531 --> 01:45:27,497
How did you know?

1501
01:45:28,000 --> 01:45:30,677
I never got a chance to tell you.

1502
01:45:44,054 --> 01:45:48,059
What about all that talk
about screwing up future events?

1503
01:45:48,183 --> 01:45:50,149
The space-time continuum?

1504
01:45:50,725 --> 01:45:53,616
Well, I figured, what the hell.

1505
01:45:53,870 --> 01:45:56,158
[Uplifting instrumental music]

1506
01:46:03,897 --> 01:46:05,776
MARTY: So how far ahead are you going?

1507
01:46:05,851 --> 01:46:08,944
About 30 years. It's a nice round number.

1508
01:46:09,030 --> 01:46:11,161
Look me up when you get there, all right?

1509
01:46:11,247 --> 01:46:13,287
Guess I'll be about 47.

1510
01:46:13,587 --> 01:46:14,664
I will.

1511
01:46:15,253 --> 01:46:16,342
MARTY: Take care.

1512
01:46:17,468 --> 01:46:18,470
You, too.

1513
01:46:18,721 --> 01:46:21,025
Right. Bye-bye, Einie.

1514
01:46:21,527 --> 01:46:24,407
MARTY: And watch that re-entry.
It's a little bumpy.

1515
01:46:25,285 --> 01:46:26,284
You bet.

1516
01:46:47,095 --> 01:46:48,636
[Explosive crashing]

1517
01:46:50,975 --> 01:46:52,767
[Dog barking]

1518
01:47:04,923 --> 01:47:06,729
[Radio plays Back In Time]

1519
01:47:31,708 --> 01:47:33,135
What a nightmare.

1520
01:47:44,329 --> 01:47:47,670
LINDA: If Paul calls, tell him I'm working
at the boutique late.

1521
01:47:47,745 --> 01:47:49,802
DAVID:
Linda, I'm not your answering service...

1522
01:47:49,925 --> 01:47:53,431
...and somebody named Greg or Craig
called you just a little while ago.

1523
01:47:53,519 --> 01:47:57,576
LINDA: Which one was it?
DAVID: I can't keep up with your boyfriends.

1524
01:47:57,987 --> 01:47:58,990
[Marty calls]

1525
01:48:00,156 --> 01:48:01,744
MARTY: What the hell is this?

1526
01:48:02,546 --> 01:48:03,624
Breakfast.

1527
01:48:04,212 --> 01:48:06,428
DAVID: Did you sleep in your clothes again?

1528
01:48:06,716 --> 01:48:07,967
Yeah. I...

1529
01:48:09,143 --> 01:48:11,437
What are you wearing, Dave?

1530
01:48:11,861 --> 01:48:12,941
Marty...

1531
01:48:13,529 --> 01:48:15,744
...I always wear a suit to the office.

1532
01:48:16,456 --> 01:48:18,172
DAVID: You all right?
MARTY: Yeah.

1533
01:48:19,091 --> 01:48:21,303
MRS. MCFLY: We need a rematch.
MR. MCFLY: A rematch?

1534
01:48:21,378 --> 01:48:23,521
-Why? Were you cheating?
-No.

1535
01:48:23,645 --> 01:48:25,350
-Hello.
-Good morning.

1536
01:48:27,564 --> 01:48:29,066
Mom! Dad!

1537
01:48:29,442 --> 01:48:31,995
MR. MCFLY: Hit your head?
MRS. MCFLY: Are you okay?

1538
01:48:32,084 --> 01:48:35,178
You guys look great.
Mom, you look so thin.

1539
01:48:35,501 --> 01:48:37,967
Why, thank you, Marty. George!

1540
01:48:39,520 --> 01:48:41,223
Good morning, sleepyhead.

1541
01:48:42,527 --> 01:48:45,281
MRS. MCFLY: Good morning, kids.
DAVID AND LINDA: 'Morning.

1542
01:48:45,407 --> 01:48:47,962
LINDA: Marty, I almost forgot.
Jennifer Parker called.

1543
01:48:48,037 --> 01:48:51,630
I sure like her. She is such a sweet girl.

1544
01:48:52,179 --> 01:48:54,308
Isn't tonight the night of the big date?

1545
01:48:54,383 --> 01:48:56,186
What? What, Ma?

1546
01:48:56,312 --> 01:49:00,319
Aren't you going up to the lake?
You've been planning it for two weeks.

1547
01:49:00,532 --> 01:49:03,073
We talked about this.
How can I go to the lake?

1548
01:49:03,285 --> 01:49:04,376
The car's wrecked.

1549
01:49:04,501 --> 01:49:06,003
MR. MCFLY: Wrecked?
DAVID: Wrecked?

1550
01:49:06,165 --> 01:49:08,384
When did this happen? Why wasn't I told?

1551
01:49:08,468 --> 01:49:12,065
MR. MCFLY: I'm sure the car is fine.
DAVID: Why am I the last to know?

1552
01:49:12,400 --> 01:49:15,232
MR. MCFLY: See? There's Biff out there
waxing it now.

1553
01:49:15,908 --> 01:49:20,216
Now, Biff, I want to make sure we get
two coats of wax, not just one.

1554
01:49:20,341 --> 01:49:24,473
-I'm just finishing up the second coat now.
-Now, Biff, don't con me.

1555
01:49:26,135 --> 01:49:28,017
I'm sorry, Mr. McFly.

1556
01:49:28,230 --> 01:49:31,108
I meant I was just starting
on the second coat.

1557
01:49:31,860 --> 01:49:34,075
Biff. What a character.

1558
01:49:34,253 --> 01:49:36,630
Always trying to get away with something.

1559
01:49:36,755 --> 01:49:39,760
I've had to stay on top of Biff
ever since high school.

1560
01:49:39,850 --> 01:49:43,943
MR. MCFLY: But, if it wasn't for him...
MRS. MCFLY: We'd never have fallen in love.

1561
01:49:45,698 --> 01:49:48,237
Mr. McFly! Mr. McFly, this just arrived.

1562
01:49:48,451 --> 01:49:51,129
Hi, Marty. I think it's your new book.

1563
01:49:53,885 --> 01:49:56,265
Honey! Your first novel.

1564
01:49:57,766 --> 01:50:02,826
MR. MCFLY: I've always told you, if you put your
mind to it, you can accomplish anything.

1565
01:50:03,037 --> 01:50:06,631
Marty, here's your keys.
You're all waxed up, ready for tonight.

1566
01:50:06,917 --> 01:50:07,832
Keys?

1567
01:50:19,829 --> 01:50:21,957
[Tender instrumental music]

1568
01:50:24,424 --> 01:50:26,889
-How about a ride, mister?
-Jennifer!

1569
01:50:28,555 --> 01:50:30,520
Are you a sight for sore eyes.

1570
01:50:30,896 --> 01:50:32,614
MARTY: Let me look at you.

1571
01:50:33,116 --> 01:50:35,994
Marty, you're acting like
you haven't seen me in a week.

1572
01:50:36,082 --> 01:50:37,170
I haven't.

1573
01:50:38,087 --> 01:50:39,172
You okay?

1574
01:50:40,090 --> 01:50:41,890
Is everything all right?

1575
01:50:44,646 --> 01:50:45,733
MARTY: Yeah.

1576
01:50:47,989 --> 01:50:49,615
Everything is great.

1577
01:50:52,621 --> 01:50:54,338
[Explosive crashing]

1578
01:50:55,589 --> 01:50:57,092
[Brakes screeching]

1579
01:51:04,064 --> 01:51:04,991
Marty!

1580
01:51:06,407 --> 01:51:08,460
You've got to come back with me!

1581
01:51:09,124 --> 01:51:11,339
-Where?
-Back to the future.

1582
01:51:16,184 --> 01:51:17,990
What are you doing, Doc?

1583
01:51:19,364 --> 01:51:20,531
I need fuel.

1584
01:51:23,873 --> 01:51:26,003
Go ahead. Quick! Get in the car.

1585
01:51:26,377 --> 01:51:30,847
No, no. I just got here. Jennifer's here.
We're taking the new truck for a spin.

1586
01:51:30,936 --> 01:51:33,815
DOC: Bring her along. This concerns her, too.

1587
01:51:33,941 --> 01:51:37,448
What are you talking about?
What happens to us in the future?

1588
01:51:37,745 --> 01:51:40,715
-Do we become assholes or something?
-No, Marty.

1589
01:51:40,789 --> 01:51:44,134
Both you and Jennifer turn out fine.
It's your kids, Marty.

1590
01:51:44,222 --> 01:51:46,436
Something has got to be done
about your kids.

1591
01:51:52,873 --> 01:51:55,878
We better back up.
We don't have enough road to get up to 88.

1592
01:51:56,003 --> 01:51:59,597
Roads? Where we're going,
we don't need roads.

1593
01:52:00,305 --> 01:53:00,811
